window.REALTEST_DATA = {"id":6,"title":"Time Mode 1 · Dojo bổ sung","provider":"Tutorials Dojo","source":"DOJOS/Sections/TIME_MODE/📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo.html","minutes":92,"supplementary":true,"questions":[{"id":1,"sourceNumber":1,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer at a multinational corporation is in charge of setting up a system for managing extensive organizational data. The system must be capable of granular access control at multiple levels: database, table, column, row, and cell. The solution must be implemented with minimal operational overhead. The company already uses Database audit logging to track access to sensitive data, but a more comprehensive access control mechanism is required.</p>\n<p>Which option meets these requirements?</p>","answers":["Implement Amazon RDS with IAM database authentication to manage fine-grained access at various levels.","Implement an Amazon Aurora database as a catalog and control access using SQL GRANTs at different levels.","Establish a data lake and a data catalog using AWS Lake Formation, controlling access via Lake Formation data filters.","Deploy a Hive metastore using Amazon EMR and manage user access through HiveQL data definition language statements."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>AWS Lake Formation is the optimal choice for setting up a centralized metadata storage solution that demands fine-grained access control. Utilizing Lake Formation data filters, a data engineer can enforce security measures at the database, table, column, row, and cell levels. This capability, coupled with its scalability and reliability, makes Lake Formation the ideal solution with minimal operational overhead.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/81d1d3a867ae85f70961f120ee01e0ef6d496787cd1a7e843c30a3d627a90eae.jpg\" alt=\"Registering an Amazon S3 location in AWS Lake Formation\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Establish a data lake and a data catalog using AWS Lake Formation, controlling access via Lake Formation data filters.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement Amazon RDS with IAM database authentication to manage fine-grained access at various levels</strong></span> is incorrect. Although Amazon RDS supports IAM database authentication for access management, it does not inherently provide the level of detailed control (specifically at the row and cell levels) required in this scenario. This limitation just makes it less suitable than Lake Formation for managing intricate permissions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement an Amazon Aurora database as a catalog and control access using SQL GRANTs at different levels</strong></span> is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora allows extensive access control through SQL GRANTs, it lacks the native capability to manage permissions at the row and cell levels without additional tools or configurations. Furthermore, this approach involves significant operational management, especially if integrating with a Hive metastore.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Deploy a Hive metastore using Amazon EMR and manage user access through HiveQL data definition language statements</strong></span> is incorrect. Deploying a Hive metastore on Amazon EMR does not simply offer the necessary granularity for permissions control at the row and cell levels. Moreover, using HiveQL for access management does not align with the requirement for minimal operational overhead.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-dl-tutorial.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-dl-tutorial.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>"},{"id":2,"sourceNumber":3,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company that provides streaming services operates several microservices. These handle various tasks such as recommending content, managing user profiles, and collecting feedback. Each microservice consumes TLS-encrypted streaming data from a specific Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK) topic.</p>\n<p>However, after the deployment of a new recommendation algorithm, the microservice for content recommendation started receiving data meant for feedback collection. This has led to inconsistencies in the user experience and skewed analytics.</p>\n<p>What solution would ensure that each microservice can only access data from its designated topic?</p>","answers":["Update the security group configurations associated with each Elastic Network Interface (ENI) of the Kafka brokers within the cluster. Add a security group rule that allows specific microservices to access their designated brokers.","Modify the partitioning key in the producer application to prevent data intended for feedback collection from being written to the content recommendation topic.","Set up Amazon MSK to utilize Kafka access control lists (ACLs). Restrict the permissions of each microservice to only its respective Amazon MSK topic.","Deploy individual source connectors for each microservice using Kafka Connect and modify the connector configurations to link each to its specific Amazon MSK topic."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK</strong>) is a fully managed service that enables you to build and run applications that use Apache Kafka to process streaming data. Amazon MSK provides the control-plane operations, such as those for creating, updating, and deleting clusters. It lets you use Apache Kafka data-plane operations, such as those for producing and consuming data. It runs open-source versions of Apache Kafka. This means existing applications, tooling, and plugins from partners and the Apache Kafka community are supported without requiring changes to application code.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/103456a9049a79f869de1a254dc0e320ac3d0cae979a310aae7e63a467803567.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Apache Kafka's Access Control Lists (ACLs) offer a highly granular approach to permissions management. They provide a mechanism to regulate which applications can read or write to a certain topic.&nbsp;They follow the format \"Principal P is [Allowed/Denied] Operation O From Host H on any Resource R matching ResourcePattern RP\". If RP doesn't match a specific resource R, then R has no associated ACLs, and therefore, no one other than super users is allowed to access R. To change this Apache Kafka behavior, you set the property&nbsp;<code>allow.everyone.if.no.acl.found</code>&nbsp;to true. Amazon MSK sets it to true by default. This means that with Amazon MSK clusters, if you don't explicitly set ACLs on a resource, all principals can access this resource. If you enable ACLs on a resource, only the authorized principals can access it. If you want to restrict access to a topic and authorize a client using TLS mutual authentication, add ACLs using the Apache Kafka authorizer CLI.</p>\n<p>In the scenario, after deploying a new recommendation algorithm, the content recommendation microservice began receiving data intended for feedback collection. This led to inconsistencies in user experience and skewed analytics. The solution to guarantee that each microservice can only access data from its assigned topic is to configure Amazon MSK to use Kafka access control lists (ACLs). By limiting the permissions of each microservice to its respective Amazon MSK topic only, the company can ensure that each microservice only accesses the data it is supposed to, thereby preventing any cross-contamination of data across different microservices. This approach provides a robust and secure method for managing access to data in a microservices architecture.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set up Amazon MSK to utilize Kafka access control lists (ACLs). Restrict the permissions of each microservice to only its respective Amazon MSK topic.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Update the security group configurations associated with each Elastic Network Interface (ENI) of the Kafka brokers within the cluster. Add a security group rule that allows specific microservices to access their designated brokers</strong></span><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect.&nbsp;Security groups in AWS are used to control inbound and outbound network traffic to AWS resources, and they operate at the level of network interfaces. While they can restrict which IP addresses can connect to the Kafka brokers, they can’t control access at the level of individual topics. Therefore, this wouldn’t prevent a misconfigured microservice from consuming data from the wrong topic.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Modify the partitioning key in the producer application to prevent data intended for feedback collection from being written to the content recommendation topic</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. The partitioning key determines how messages are distributed among the various partitions within a Kafka topic. Modifying the partitioning key in the producer application would change the distribution of the messages, but it would not alter or restrict which microservices can access which topics</p>\n<p>The option says: <span><strong>Deploy individual source connectors for each microservice using Kafka Connect and modify the connector configurations to link each to its specific Amazon MSK topic</strong><strong>&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Kafka Connect is designed to bridge Kafka with other systems, such as databases or search systems. Its main purpose is to enable data flow between Kafka and other platforms, not to enforce data access controls</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-acls.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-acls.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/what-is-msk.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/what-is-msk.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon MKS Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka-amazon-msk/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka-amazon-msk/</a></p>"},{"id":3,"sourceNumber":5,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer at a large organization is setting up an AWS Glue job to transform and load data from various sources into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. To ensure security and access management, the engineer needs to store and access the necessary credentials securely.</p>\n<p>What is the most secure and best practice for the engineer to manage these credentials?</p>","answers":["Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve secret values.","Use AWS Parameter Store to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve parameters.","Create a configuration file containing the credentials and upload it to an Amazon S3 bucket. Update the bucket policy to grant the Glue job access to the file.","Pass the credentials as job parameters when invoking the AWS Glue job."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Secrets Manager is a robust service designed specifically for managing access to applications, services, and IT resources. It provides a secure and scalable method to store and manage database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. This service is particularly useful in scenarios where there is a need to securely handle sensitive information such as credentials. It allows you to protect access to your applications by enabling you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve these secrets throughout their lifecycle.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5acd7d3f382ab699091e40b50bae48861234da1711937ca14f9bd70d692b7add.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>When setting up an AWS Glue job, it is important to manage credentials securely. AWS Secrets Manager integrates seamlessly with other AWS services and provides a centralized service to manage secrets. By storing credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, they are kept secure and can be programmatically retrieved when needed. This eliminates the need to hardcode sensitive information in plain text, which is a common security bad practice.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4545f876ee14cfeae0958a33e0873c41a66e26757a9311c67eb4888c4cb27f93.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>In addition, AWS Secrets Manager allows for fine-grained access control to secrets using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies. This means that you can control which AWS Glue jobs or other AWS resources have permission to access these secrets. This level of control is important in maintaining the principle of least privilege, a key security best practice.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span> <strong>Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve secret values.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Parameter Store to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve parameters</strong></span> is incorrect. Systems Manager Parameter Store is also a viable option, but it is generally recommended for storing configuration data rather than sensitive credentials. It’s not the best choice for managing credentials in this scenario. AWS Secrets Manager is more suited for this purpose as it offers additional security features tailored for credential management, like automatic secrets rotation.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a configuration file containing the credentials and upload it to an Amazon S3 bucket. Update the bucket policy to grant the Glue job access to the file</strong></span> is incorrect because storing sensitive information like credentials in a plain text configuration file in an Amazon S3 bucket is not a secure practice. Even though you can control access to the S3 bucket using IAM roles, it does not provide the same level of protection and management features as AWS Secrets Manager.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Pass the credentials as job parameters when invoking the AWS Glue job</strong></span> is incorrect because hardcoding sensitive information like credentials as job parameters can lead to security risks. These parameters are also logged in AWS CloudTrail, making it a less secure option.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/security-iam.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/security-iam.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Secrets Manager Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/</a></p>\n<p><strong>AWS Secrets Manager vs Systems Manager Parameter Store:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager-vs-systems-manager-parameter-store/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager-vs-systems-manager-parameter-store/</a></p>"},{"id":4,"sourceNumber":7,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer manages a workflow where financial records are continuously uploaded to Amazon S3. These records contain confidential information that needs encryption measures to enhance security. Only a select group of employees must have control over the encryption keys.</p>\n<p>How can the data engineer fulfill the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Store encryption keys in an AWS CloudHSM cluster. Create an IAM policy to limit access. Encrypt/decrypt objects using CloudHSM for S3 write operations.","Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C). Limit access to the keys responsible for encryption.","Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Use KMS key policies to define the users who can access the encryption keys.","Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3). Create an IAM policy to control access to the S3-managed keys."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Amazon S3 provides several methods to encrypt objects stored at rest:</p>\n<p><strong>S3-managed encryption (SSE-S3):</strong> With this method, Amazon S3 encrypts your data using keys that it manages and controls. This is the default encryption for all new objects. Amazon handles the encryption, decryption, and key management for you.</p>\n<p><strong>SSE-C (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys):</strong> You manage and control the encryption keys, while Amazon S3 performs the encryption and decryption operations on your behalf. You have complete control over the keys, but Amazon handles the encryption/decryption process.</p>\n<p><strong>Server-Side Encryption with AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS):</strong> Provides an additional layer of security by integrating with AWS KMS for centralized key management and access controls. KMS encrypts the data keys, which S3 then uses to encrypt the objects.</p>\n<p><strong>Client-Side Encryption:</strong> You can encrypt objects client-side before uploading to S3 using libraries like the Amazon S3 Encryption Client. This gives you complete control over the encryption process.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f470b7e5c154eaeb36cd4a23645b8c6756e3b0c168edbdec8be3b779e6ceb0b3.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Encrypting files with confidential customer information in Amazon S3 requires a solution that not only secures the data but also allows for precise encryption key control. AWS Key Management Service (KMS) meets these needs by enabling users to create, manage, and control the use of encryption keys across AWS services and their applications.</p>\n<p>AWS KMS integrates with S3 to support server-side encryption using KMS keys. When uploading data to S3, you specify the KMS key to be used for encryption; S3 then encrypts the file before storing it. KMS key policies primarily control who can access the keys and delineate the permissions regarding what actions they can perform with these keys. Through these policies, the data engineer can precisely delegate which employees can use the keys for encrypting and decrypting the financial records stored in S3.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Use KMS key policies to define the users who can access the encryption keys.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Store encryption keys in an AWS CloudHSM cluster. Create an IAM policy to limit access. Encrypt/decrypt objects using CloudHSM for S3 write operations</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;In AWS CloudHSM, you're responsible for managing the hardware security module that generates and stores your keys. This involves significant operational overhead, including hardware provisioning, scaling, and maintenance. While CloudHSM provides a high level of security, it's more complex and costly compared to managing keys with AWS KMS.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C). Limit access to the keys responsible for encryption</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because SSE-C requires the customer to manage and secure the encryption keys outside of AWS. This increases the complexity and risk, as the customer is responsible for these keys' secure generation, storage, and rotation.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3). Create an IAM policy to control access to the S3-managed keys</strong></span> is incorrect because SSE-S3 provides a simple and managed encryption solution using Amazon S3 managed keys. However, it does not offer the same level of control or auditing over the encryption keys as SSE-KMS, making it less suitable for scenarios requiring strict access control to encryption keys.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/overview.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingKMSEncryption.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingKMSEncryption.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>"},{"id":5,"sourceNumber":8,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company has centralized its data storage in an Amazon S3 bucket. Within this repository, specific datasets include personally identifiable information (PII). Given the diversity of user groups needing access to raw data, a data engineer must ensure that each group can only access the PII relevant to them.</p>\n<p>Which solution would satisfy the requirement in the LEAST amount of operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Register the Amazon S3 location in AWS Lake Formation. Utilize Lake Formation's data filters to manage access for different users. Query data using Amazon Athena.","Use AWS Glue to create ETL jobs that transform and move the data into Amazon Redshift. Apply row-level security in Redshift for different user groups.","Create a query interface using Amazon Athena to execute backend data queries. Create user groups in Amazon Cognito and assign permission levels based on user-specific PII access requirements.","Provision IAM roles with granular access permissions and associate these roles with IAM user groups. Attach an IAM policy to the group that restricts access at the column level."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Lake Formation</strong> streamlines the process of setting up a secure data lake in AWS. It provides fine-grained access control to sensitive data stored in Amazon S3 through a simple request or revoke process, allowing organizations to define who has access to specific aspects of their data. When integrated with <strong>Amazon Athena</strong>, a serverless interactive query service, Lake Formation enables column-level security (CLS) by leveraging data filters. This approach significantly reduces the effort required to manage access to PII data, as it directly associates user access levels with their IAM roles, eliminating the need for complex custom solutions or manual intervention.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/17d0d6e4012bd290002af76c4b6a9613d522b87d43f4755e254fa1adeb57a9da.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Register the Amazon S3 location in AWS Lake Formation. Utilize Lake Formation's data filters to manage access for different users. Query data using Amazon Athena.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue to create ETL jobs that transform and move the data into Amazon Redshift. Apply row-level security in Redshift for different user groups</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach introduces additional complexity, such as the need for ETL jobs and data transfer into Redshift. Implementing row-level security in Redshift adds overhead in terms of managing the data pipeline, handling transformations, and dealing with ongoing data movement. It’s not as streamlined as using Lake Formation and Athena.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a query interface using Amazon Athena to execute backend data queries. Create user groups in Amazon Cognito and assign permission levels based on user-specific PII access requirements </strong></span>is incorrect because it mainly suggests building a custom solution, which inherently requires more effort than using AWS's managed services designed for security and access control. While integrating Athena and Cognito is feasible, the development, maintenance, and scalability of custom UIs typically demand significantly more resources.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Provision IAM roles with granular access permissions and associate these roles with IAM user groups. Attach an IAM policy to the group that restricts access at the column level </strong></span>is incorrect because it primarily uses IAM roles and identity-based policies for column-level access, which isn't directly supported by IAM for S3 resources.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/what-is-amazon-cognito.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/what-is-amazon-cognito.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>"},{"id":6,"sourceNumber":9,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer wants to use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results of Amazon Athena queries, which are delivered in an S3 bucket. However, the data engineer encountered a permission-related error message when they attempted to access the QuickSight dashboard.</p>\n<p>What could be the underlying reasons for this error? (Select THREE.)</p>","answers":["QuickSight does not have a configured connection to Athena.","Athena data tables have not been properly cataloged, leading to potential access issues within QuickSight.","QuickSight is not authorized to access the S3 bucket containing the data.","QuickSight is not authorized to decrypt the data in the S3 bucket, preventing data access.","The data engineer's IAM user does not have the required read and write access to the S3 bucket.","Amazon Athena's query performance optimization settings are not properly configured."],"correct":[2,3,4],"explanation":"<p>The following are common reasons for getting <strong>Access denied</strong> errors when you use Amazon Athena as a data source in Amazon QuickSight:</p>\n<p>- Your QuickSight account doesn't have the required permission to access the Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket.</p>\n<p>- Your data file is encrypted with an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key.</p>\n<p>- You don't have the necessary AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions policy assigned to you.</p>\n<p>- The Amazon S3 bucket doesn't exist, or the IAM role used to query the data doesn't have the required S3 permissions.</p>\n<p>- You don't have the necessary service control policies (SCPs) assigned to you. (For QuickSight accounts that use AWS Organizations)</p>\n<p>- Your QuickSight user or group doesn't have AWS Lake Formation permissions. (For Athena accounts that use Lake Formation)</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/14fca410b711a042813fefb20420c4646f404640215cfc293839f00ab035af3b.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>QuickSight uses a service role to communicate with other AWS services like Amazon Athena and Amazon S3 on your behalf. If you are being denied access to a QuickSight dashboard, it's most likely due to insufficient permissions related to the QuickSight service role. For the data engineer to successfully run queries in Athena, their IAM user must have the necessary permissions to access the S3 bucket where Athena stores the query results, as well as permissions to decrypt the data if the S3 bucket is encrypted. IAM provides managed policies that you can use to avoid guessing required permissions and save time when granting access.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>- <strong>QuickSight is not authorized to access the S3 bucket containing the data.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>- <strong>QuickSight is not authorized to decrypt the data in the S3 bucket, preventing data access.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>-</span><span><strong>The data engineer's IAM user does not have the required read and write access to the S3 bucket.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>QuickSight does not have a configured connection to Athena </strong></span>is incorrect because it simply addresses the configuration for data querying between QuickSight and Athena, not the permissions issue directly. Therefore. this option is not related to the error.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Athena data tables have not been properly cataloged, leading to potential access issues within QuickSight </span></strong>is incorrect. This option primarily focuses on the organization and accessibility of data within Athena. While cataloging data tables is crucial for data querying, the permissions error encountered in QuickSight is tied to access rights, not data organization. Proper cataloging would not resolve a permissions denial error when attempting to access data.</p>\n<p><span>The option that says:<span><strong> Amazon Athena's query performance optimization settings are not properly configured</strong></span></span><span> is incorrect because it only addresses Athena's query performance optimization rather than permissions. Athena's query performance optimization settings pertain to the efficiency and speed of data processing and have no direct impact on permissions or access rights.</span></p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-access-denied-athena-data\">https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-access-denied-athena-data</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/troubleshoot-athena-insufficient-permissions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/troubleshoot-athena-insufficient-permissions.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon Quicksight and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>"},{"id":7,"sourceNumber":11,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company is developing a containerized application to transform data using containers orchestrated by Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS), operating on Amazon EC2 nodes. The containers run as Pods on the EC2 nodes. In this setup, a data engineer faces the task of selecting an appropriate storage solution for the data before its transformation. Each container will process distinct datasets and subsequently store the transformed data in a data lake. Data sharing among the containers is prohibited.</p>\n<p>Which storage solution offers the LOWEST latency for this scenario?</p>","answers":["Utilize an ephemeral volume provided by the node's RAM for the containers.","Configure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX).","<span>Have containers use a <code>PersistentVolume</code> backed by Amazon EFS, managed through AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK).</span>","Configure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon MemoryDB for Redis."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Using an ephemeral volume provided by the node's RAM in Amazon EKS is the most efficient approach for achieving the lowest latency. Amazon EKS, a managed Kubernetes service, orchestrates container deployment on nodes, which can be EC2 instances or managed through AWS Fargate. Ephemeral volumes, tied to the pod's lifecycle, can swiftly access the node's local resources like drives or RAM. Since the requirement does not involve data sharing between containers and focuses on low-latency access, local storage like node RAM is ideal.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/e760e6d58d5665a009780c6e0eaaeb2ed55972643b707f9783857e5016ec56df.jpg\" alt=\"ephemeral volume\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize an ephemeral volume provided by the node's RAM for the containers.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)</strong></span> is incorrect. Although DAX, a write-through cache for DynamoDB, offers low-latency access to stored items, it typically requires network calls for storage access. This external storage setup introduces more latency compared to local node storage.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Have containers use a <code>PersistentVolume</code> backed by Amazon EFS, managed through AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK)</strong></span> is incorrect. While AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK) allow for efficient management of AWS services within the Kubernetes environment, using a PersistentVolume backed by Amazon EFS introduces higher latency. This is primarily due to the network calls required for accessing EFS, making it less suitable for scenarios where low latency is crucial.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon MemoryDB for Redis</strong></span> is incorrect. While MemoryDB provides low-latency in-memory database services, it requires network connectivity for data access. Like DAX, being external to the node means increased latency compared to local storage options.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/storage.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/storage.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/persistent-storage-for-kubernetes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/persistent-storage-for-kubernetes/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-compute.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-compute.html<br>\n</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-elastic-kubernetes-service-eks/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-elastic-kubernetes-service-eks/</a></p>"},{"id":8,"sourceNumber":12,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data analytics company is using AWS Glue for its ETL jobs to process and transform large-scale data sets. The team’s Glue ETL jobs are built using PySpark scripts to perform distributed data processing and transformations across large-scale datasets. The team is experiencing irregularities and inefficiencies in its job execution times and is seeking to optimize it. To solve this, the team wants to accurately and effectively identify the root causes of these Glue jobs.</p>\n<p>Which method should the team use to gather the MOST detailed data and analyses for these operations?</p>","answers":["Use AWS CloudTrail's Data Events to monitor and log data access and API activity for the Glue ETL jobs.","Query the Glue Data Catalog using Amazon Athena for real-time monitoring of Glue job metrics and performance analysis.","Profile and monitor Glue operations using Glue Monitoring and the Glue Job profiler through Amazon CloudWatch.","Use AWS CloudFormation templates to review and manage the configuration of the Glue ETL job resources."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a managed ETL service that facilitates the extraction, transformation, and loading of data for analysis. It automates and simplifies data integration processes, making it easier to prepare and consolidate data for analytics.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f73bc9b7b375cf33ec66532d0b97bb3015d0d6e138729026c76bc2a966b052a3.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue job run monitoring dashboard\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue offers a Job run monitoring feature that gives a quick overview of job runs, including status, timestamps, and other key details. For deeper insights, the AWS Glue Job Profiler goes a step further, offering detailed metrics such as execution time, data processing rates, and memory usage. These metrics are crucial for pinpointing performance bottlenecks and fine-tuning resource utilization in ETL jobs.</p>\n<p>When these features are integrated with Amazon CloudWatch, they create dedicated log groups for each Glue job. These logs in CloudWatch provide a granular view of job performance. For example, by analyzing log data, teams can track how long specific data transformation tasks take or identify recurring errors in job runs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/11710a779a4faeeff902d5508af117fc4df306236ccd5dd747baa8fc97cac799.png\" alt=\"Amazon CloudWatch log group details and active log streams for AWS Glue jobs\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong>&nbsp;Profile and monitor Glue operations using Glue Monitoring and the Glue Job profiler through Amazon CloudWatch.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS CloudTrail's Data Events to monitor and log data access and API activity for the Glue ETL jobs</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS CloudTrail's Data Events are primarily used for recording data plane activities of some AWS resources, such as object uploads to Amazon S3, Lambda Function invocations, or item deletions in a DynamoDB table. While they can log some API activity for AWS Glue, they are not designed for in-depth performance monitoring or analysis of Glue jobs, especially in comparison to the detailed metrics available through the AWS Glue job profiler and CloudWatch.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Query the Glue Data Catalog using Amazon Athena for real-time monitoring of Glue job metrics and performance analysis</strong></span> is incorrect because it is inefficient for real-time performance monitoring. Querying the AWS Glue Data Catalog with Athena provides access to metadata but does not offer the same level of real-time, detailed job execution metrics and analysis capabilities as the AWS Glue job profiler and CloudWatch.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS CloudFormation templates to review and manage the configuration of the Glue ETL job resources</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS CloudFormation is used for infrastructure deployment and resource management, not for analyzing AWS Glue job execution performance or identifying the root causes of ETL job inefficiencies.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-profile-glue-job-cloudwatch-metrics.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-profile-glue-job-cloudwatch-metrics.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/security-logging-monitoring.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/security-logging-monitoring.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":9,"sourceNumber":13,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A healthcare company has recently started using Amazon Glue DataBrew for data preparation tasks, moving away from previous pipelines that relied on an AWS Lambda user-defined function for custom data masking. A dataset containing patient information, which includes Personally Identifiable Information (PII), is given for processing. To comply with data privacy regulations, the PII data needs to be masked before further processing. The technique chosen for this task should replace the actual PII data with other authentic-looking values to maintain the overall structure and statistical distribution of the data.</p>\n<p>Which of the following data-masking techniques should be used?</p>","answers":["Substitution","Decryption","Probabilistic encryption","Nulling out or deletion"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Substitution</strong> is a data-masking technique that involves replacing actual PII data with other authentic-looking values. This technique is useful in maintaining the overall structure and statistical distribution of the data, which is important for testing or development purposes. For example, a patient’s real name in a healthcare dataset could be replaced with a fictitious but real-sounding name. This way, the sensitive information is protected, but the data remains useful for analysis or testing.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/628dfe583a2d0808f4b08b15c75dcc947054415cb8dbe803fe8f5f60bbb74b28.jpg\" alt=\"AWS Glue DataBrew job run history for a PII masking recipe\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Substitution.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Decryption</strong></span> is a process used to convert encrypted data back into its original form. It requires a specific key, which is used to decrypt the encrypted data. In the context of data privacy, decryption would be used to access the original data, not to mask or protect it.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Probabilistic encryption</strong></span> is a type of encryption that produces different ciphertext each time it’s applied, even for the same plaintext. This means that if you encrypt the same data twice, you’ll get two different ciphertexts. While this adds an extra layer of security, it’s not the same as replacing PII data with other authentic-looking values. It’s a way to protect data, but it doesn’t maintain the same structure or statistical distribution of the data.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Nulling out or deletion</strong></span> is a data-masking technique where a particular field is replaced with a null value or the column is deleted entirely. While this does protect sensitive information, it doesn’t replace the PII data with other authentic-looking values. Instead, it removes the data entirely, which can impact the utility of the dataset for testing or development purposes.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/personal-information-protection.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/personal-information-protection.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-masking/\">https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-masking/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":10,"sourceNumber":14,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer is assigned to build a data lake for a company’s business data stored on Amazon S3. The data, saved in the Apache Parquet format, will be utilized for complex analytic tasks. The company anticipates that users will need granular access to the datasets.</p>\n<p>Which of the following will meet these requirements in the shortest amount of time and in the MOST cost-effective manner?</p>","answers":["Execute an AWS Glue crawler to establish a data catalog for existing data and register its Amazon S3 path location on Lake Formation. For data security, allocate granular permissions to various users.","Leverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows. Ensure data security by assigning appropriate access controls.","Using AWS Lake Formation, register the S3 bucket where the data resides. To secure the data, assign granular permissions to different users.","Import data from Amazon S3 using an AWS Lake Formation blueprint. Subsequently, accord users granular Lake Formation permissions as needed."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Lake Formation</strong> is a service that makes it easy to set up a secure data lake in days. A data lake is a centralized, curated, and secured repository that stores all your data, both in its original form and prepared for analysis. A data lake enables you to break down data silos and combine different types of analytics to gain insights and guide better business decisions.</p>\n<p>Amazon S3 forms the storage layer for Lake Formation. If you already use S3, you typically begin by registering existing S3 buckets that contain your data. Lake Formation creates new buckets for the data lake and imports data into them. AWS always stores this data in your account, and only you have direct access to it.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/88b2714812a8d8367da385e92999efc03a3553eca13595b31fc340bffae1bab3.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Lake Formation is integrated with AWS Glue, which you can use to create a data catalog that describes available datasets and their appropriate business applications.&nbsp;Lake Formation lets you define policies and control data access with simple \"grant and revoke permissions to data\" sets at granular levels.&nbsp;You can assign permissions to IAM users, roles, groups, and Active Directory users using federation. You specify permissions on catalog objects (like tables and columns) rather than on buckets and objects.</p>\n<p>AWS Lake Formation also provides blueprints that you can run for loading and cataloging data.&nbsp;A blueprint allows you to&nbsp;import data from MySQL, Postgres, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Oracle databases running in Amazon RDS or hosted in Amazon EC2. With Lake Formation, you can import data from MySQL, Postgres, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Oracle databases running in Amazon RDS or hosted in Amazon EC2. You can also import from on-premises databases by connecting&nbsp;with Java Database Connectivity (JDBC).</p>\n<p>It was indicated in the scenario that data in Parquet format is already existing in Amazon S3. As a result, you don't need to utilize a Lake Formation blueprint.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Execute an AWS Glue crawler to establish a data catalog for existing data and register its Amazon S3 path location on Lake Formation. For data security, allocate granular permissions to various users.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Leverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows. Ensure data security by assigning appropriate access controls</strong></span><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because while Amazon MWAA can orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows, it does not provide a solution for creating a data lake or managing granular access to datasets. These tasks would need to be handled separately, which could increase complexity and cost.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Using AWS Lake Formation, register the S3 bucket where the data resides. To secure the data, assign granular permissions to different users</strong></span><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. You should register the Amazon S3 path of the bucket where your data is stored and not the bucket name.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Import data from Amazon S3 using an AWS Lake Formation blueprint. Subsequently, accord users granular Lake Formation permissions as needed</strong></span><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because Amazon S3 is not a valid import source in a Lake Formation blueprint.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-securing-and-managing-data-lakes-with-aws-lake-formation/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-securing-and-managing-data-lakes-with-aws-lake-formation/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>"},{"id":11,"sourceNumber":15,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company uses a variety of SaaS applications and AWS services. The data engineering team has been tasked with integrating and automating data flows between Salesforce and Amazon Redshift.</p>\n<p>The team needs to transfer new Salesforce event data to Redshift every day but also requires the ability to manually trigger the data flow for debugging and testing purposes. The data transferred should only include records that have been updated or newly created since the last successful flow run, similar to how the team's existing Glue ETL pipelines use AWS Glue job bookmarks to process only new and updated data from other sources.</p>\n<p>Which Amazon AppFlow Flow Trigger should be used?</p>","answers":["On-demand flows","Event-triggered flows","Full transfer schedule-triggered flows","Incremental transfer schedule-triggered flows"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>When you select incremental transfer, Amazon AppFlow transfers only the records that have been added or changed since the last successful flow run. This is achieved by selecting a source timestamp field to specify how Amazon AppFlow identifies new or changed records. For example, if you have a Created Date timestamp field, you can instruct Amazon AppFlow to transfer only newly-created records (and not changed records) since the last successful flow run.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/32294cbf595193ed869c86e8055104cf40d5b070b9b83a7b5131279abdffcef2.png\" alt=\"Amazon AppFlow data integration workflow from SaaS sources to cloud destinations\"></p>\n<p>The first schedule-triggered flow will pull 30 days of past records at the time of the first flow run. To transfer records created or modified over a different time range than the past 30 days at the time of the first flow run, you can set up the flow to be triggered on demand. You can then use the filter option to pull records over the desired time range. After the on-demand flow runs and pulls the initial set of records, you can edit the flow to be triggered on schedule so that subsequent flow runs transfer incremental data.</p>\n<p>Optionally, you can add a time offset to the time range for the incremental transfer. The flow run will import records that were created or changed between the previous flow run and the specified offset prior to the current flow run. This feature can be used to accommodate any latencies in the source systems in timestamping changes to records.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Incremental transfer schedule-triggered flows.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>On-demand flows </strong></span>is incorrect.&nbsp;These flows are manually run as needed. This option would not be suitable for the given scenario because the requirement is to transfer new Salesforce event data to Redshift every day automatically, not manually.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Event-triggered flows</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon AppFlow runs these flows based on a specified change event in the source application. This option is not suitable for the given scenario because the requirement is to transfer data every day, not just when a change event occurs.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Full transfer schedule-triggered flows</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon AppFlow runs these flows based on the schedule that you specify during the flow setup. When you select full transfer, Amazon AppFlow transfers a snapshot of all records at the time of the flow run from the source to the destination. This option is not suitable for the given scenario because the requirement is to transfer only the records that have been added or changed since the last successful flow run, not all records.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/flow-triggers.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/flow-triggers.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/1.0/APIReference/API_ScheduledTriggerProperties.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/1.0/APIReference/API_ScheduledTriggerProperties.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon AppFlow Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/</a></p>"},{"id":12,"sourceNumber":16,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>An organization plans to ingest data from external software-as-a-service (SaaS) applications and store it in an Amazon S3 bucket. The organization will then analyze the data using Amazon Redshift.</p>\n<p>Which AWS service would meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Amazon AppFlow","Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)","AWS Step Functions","Amazon EventBridge"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon AppFlow</strong> is a fully managed integration service that enables secure, private data transfer between AWS services and SaaS applications with minimal operational overhead. It allows for the direct integration of SaaS applications like Salesforce, ServiceNow, Slack, and many others with AWS services.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/b7e1361174f3ea9b8065737e40dbf85c912517b1497ce47599186f2c6b3a1ef5.png\"></p>\n<p>Amazon AppFlow simplifies the process of securely transferring data between SaaS applications and AWS services, thereby significantly reducing the operational overhead associated with data integration tasks. It supports various data transformation capabilities, which means that data can be formatted and transformed as needed during the transfer process, ensuring that the data stored in Amazon S3 is ready for analysis in Amazon Redshift.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Amazon AppFlow.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon MWAA is a managed service for orchestrating complex workflows using Apache Airflow. It requires more operational overhead compared to Amazon AppFlow for the use case of simply transferring data from SaaS applications to AWS services.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Step Functions</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS Step Functions is primarily used for coordinating multiple AWS services into serverless workflows. While it can handle complex workflows involving AWS services and external systems, it introduces unnecessary complexity and operational overhead for the specific task of transferring data from SaaS applications to Amazon S3.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon EventBridge</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus service designed for application integration and event-driven computing. Although it can route data from various sources to AWS services, it is not specifically optimized for the direct transfer of data from SaaS applications to AWS services like Amazon AppFlow is.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/appflow/\">https://aws.amazon.com/appflow/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon AppFlow Cheat Sheet</strong>:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/</a></p>"},{"id":13,"sourceNumber":19,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A retail company handles a large amount of data in their Amazon Redshift. The company wants to find all the product names that start with “TAD” or “BAG.” Additionally, they want to process the data, which will be used for creating reports in the fastest way possible.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;What query would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["<span><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name LIKE 'TAD%' OR product_name LIKE 'BAG%';</code></span>","<span><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name ~ '^TAD|BAG';</code></span>","<span><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name SIMILAR TO '(TAD|BAG)%';</code></span>","<span><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name !~ '^TAD|BAG';</code></span>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>A pattern-matching operator searches a string for a pattern specified in the conditional expression and returns true or false depending on whether it finds a match. Amazon Redshift uses three methods for pattern matching:</p>\n<div>\n<p><code>LIKE</code> Operator where it compares a string expression, and its pattern-matching covers the entire string.</p>\n<p><code>SIMILAR TO</code> Operator where it matches a string expression with a SQL standard regular expression pattern.</p>\n<p><code>POSIX</code>&nbsp;Operator that provides a more powerful means for pattern matching since it can match any portion of the string and performs a case-sensitive match.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/228befb8442b1cbe75b192fa5dfe6afea0c2e63ed02018d4537322a4786bf4f2.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Using <code>SIMILAR TO</code> or <code>POSIX</code> operators are computationally expensive when doing regular expression matching. Furthermore, <code>LIKE</code> the command performs way better compared to the other operators as it does not use <code>REGEX</code>, which causes potential drawbacks.</p>\n<p>Hence, The correct option is: <span><strong><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name LIKE 'TAD%' OR product_name LIKE 'BAG%';</code></strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong><code></code></strong></span><br>\nThe option that says:<span> <strong><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name ~ '^TAD|BAG';</code></strong></span> is incorrect. This query uses <code>POSIX</code> regex with the <code>~</code> operator to match product names. While this archives the same result as the <code>LIKE</code> operator, using <code>REGEX</code> is generally slower and computationally expensive.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<span> <strong><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name SIMILAR TO '(TAD|BAG)%';</code></strong></span> is incorrect. This query uses the <code>SIMILAR TO</code> operator, an SQL standard, for pattern matching. However, this is less efficient and computationally expensive as this operator internally translates to a <code>REGEX</code>, causing performance drawbacks.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong><code>SELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name !~ '^TAD|BAG';</code></strong> </span>is incorrect. This query uses the <code>POSIX</code> regex operator for negation pattern matching. This operator will find product names that do not match the specific pattern, making it incorrect to find patterns. Additionally, <code>REGEX</code> operators are less efficient for pattern matching than the <code>LIKE</code> operator.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions-posix.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions-posix.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong><br>\n<a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n</div>"},{"id":14,"sourceNumber":20,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company operates a MySQL database in a data center. Recently, they have decided to replicate their data into an Amazon S3 bucket for analysis. The company must ensure that ongoing changes in its database are reflected in the target S3 bucket.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;Which solution can satisfy the requirements? (Select TWO).</p>","answers":["Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with Full Load + change data capture (CDC).","Use Apache Parquet format when writing the data into the S3 bucket.","Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with change data capture (CDC) Only.","Use CSV format when writing the data into the S3 bucket.","Use AWS Lambda to trigger data replication on a daily basis from the MySQL database to the S3 bucket."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Database Migration Service(AWS DMS) is a cloud service that makes it possible to migrate relational database, data warehouses, NoSQL databases, and other data stores.</p>\n<p>You can use AWS DMS to transfer data to Amazon S3 from any database source. When Amazon S3 is employed as a target in an AWS DMS task, both full load and change data capture(CDC) data are written to a comma-separated value (.csv) format by default. However, to ensure more compact storage and faster query performance, you may opt to have the data written in Apache Parquet(.parquet) format.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/398e4a7b657a7e2dfb9b0251d60b19936a7d36648d5749eea0076b71cd6af088.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>When using Full load + CDC in AWS DMZ Task, the task migrates existing data and then updates the target database based on changes to the source database.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct options are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with Full Load + CDC.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Use Apache Parquet format when writing the data into the S3 bucket.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong><span>Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with CDC Only</span>&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect it is because, by default, When using Amazon S3 as a target in an AWS DMS task, both full load and CDC data are used. However, choosing the CDC needs to pay more attention to the initial full load of the existing data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use CSV format when writing the data into the S3 bucket</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. While you can use CSV format when writing the data, there are better choices for optimal query performance, especially when dealing with extensive data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Lambda to trigger data replication on a daily basis from the MySQL database to the S3 bucket </strong></span>is incorrect. While AWS Lambda can be used for data movement tasks, its batch-processing nature could lead to significant delays in reflecting the latest data changes. Additionally, the manual effort required to manage and scale the replication gives an additional amount of operational overhead.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Database Migration Service Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/</a></p>"},{"id":15,"sourceNumber":22,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A startup has a Salesforce-powered SaaS application with a data warehouse running in Amazon Redshift. As part of its workflow, the company needs to set up an automated solution to continuously send encrypted data from its SaaS solution to its Redshift data warehouse. This process is required to meet the company's data policy. Additionally, the data engineer must ensure that the company retains full control over its encrypted data. The team considered using Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to trigger a custom ingestion job but found that a direct connector would reduce operational overhead.</p>\n<p>Which of the following meets the requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Create a new workflow in Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) and set up the Salesforce solution to forward the data to MWAA via the Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector in Redshift. Use an AWS-managed key for encrypting connection data.","Set up an AWS Glue Workflow to orchestrate a Crawler and ETL Job between the Salesforce application and Redshift. Configure a rule using EventBridge and define the event pattern that will trigger the Glue Workflow as the target.","<span>Launch a user-defined function (UDF) in Redshift with a <code>Run on demand</code> flow trigger. Configure the UDF to synchronize the data exchange between the Salesforce solution and the Redshift data warehouse. </span>","<span>Use an Amazon AppFlow flow with a <code>Run on event</code> trigger. Configure it to ingest data from the Salesforce solution to the Redshift data warehouse. Use customer-managed key for encrypting connection data.</span>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Amazon AppFlow is a fully managed integration service that enables you to securely exchange data between software as a service (SaaS) applications, such as Salesforce, and AWS services, such as Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and Amazon Redshift. For example, you can ingest contact records from Salesforce to Amazon Redshift or pull support tickets from Zendesk to an Amazon S3 bucket.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/b721dcd19152e1135746a96b610b7255b8d3e5efd5c511818b9ccae77046b735.png\" alt=\"Configure flow page in Amazon AppFlow setting Salesforce as the source and Amazon Redshift as the destination.\"></p>\n<p>AppFlow relieves you from investing significant time and highly skilled developer staff to build and maintain custom API connectors for AWS services to exchange data with SaaS applications. Systems Administrators and business analysts can quickly implement most of the integrations they need without waiting months for IT to finish integration projects.</p>\n<p>A trigger determines how a flow runs. The following are the supported flow trigger types:</p>\n<p><strong>Run on demand</strong> — Users manually run the flow as needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Run on event</strong> — Amazon AppFlow runs the flow in response to an event from a SaaS application.</p>\n<p><strong>Run on schedule</strong> — Amazon AppFlow runs the flow on a recurring schedule.</p>\n<p>Amazon AppFlow provides both AWS-managed and customer-managed keys for encrypting connection data and data stored in Amazon S3 when it is a destination. We recommend that you use customer-managed keys, as it puts you in full control over your encrypted data. When you choose customer-managed keys, Amazon AppFlow attaches a resource policy to the KMS key that grants it access to the KMS key.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Use an Amazon AppFlow flow with a <code>Run on event</code> trigger. Configure it to ingest data from the Salesforce solution to the Redshift data warehouse. Use customer-managed key for encrypting connection data.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Launch a user-defined function (UDF) in Redshift with a <code>Run on demand</code> flow trigger. Configure the UDF to synchronize the data exchange between the Salesforce solution and the Redshift data warehouse</strong></span> is incorrect because UDFs simply have certain limitations in terms of accessing external systems or services that are outside of AWS, such as a custom SaaS solution. In addition, a UDF is not capable of continuously sending data from the SaaS application to the Redshift data warehouse. It's also important to note that the&nbsp;<code>Run on demand</code> flow trigger is commonly used in Amazon AppFlow and not in UDF.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a new workflow in Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) and set up the Salesforce solution to forward the data to MWAA via the Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector in Redshift. Use an AWS-managed key for encrypting connection data</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Although using Amazon MWAA can satisfy the given requirement of continuously sending data from the SaaS application to Amazon Redshift, it still entails a lot of operational overhead to establish the end-to-end connection. Take note that the scenario explicitly asks for the least amount of overhead. Moreover, the use of AWS-managed keys does not fulfill the goal of providing the company with full control over its encrypted data. A customer-managed key should be used in this scenario instead.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Set up an AWS Glue Workflow to orchestrate a Crawler and ETL Job between the Salesforce application and Redshift. Configure a rule using EventBridge and define the event pattern that will trigger the Glue Workflow as the target</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Glue Workflows is primarily used to create and visualize complex ETL activities. It is not for integrating an external SaaS solution with an AWS Service. Using Eventbridge requires an intermediary service to write the output data to Amazon Redshift. This solution would not continuously send data from the SaaS application to Amazon Redshift with the LEAST operational overhead.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/appflow/\">https://aws.amazon.com/appflow/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon AppFlow Cheat Sheet</strong>:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/</a></p>"},{"id":16,"sourceNumber":23,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company uses an Amazon Redshift cluster with KEY distribution for its data warehouse. This cluster has multiple nodes, but one node is using more CPU than the others, which have normal CPU usage during business hours.</p>\n<p>Due to a limited budget, the data engineering team seeks a solution to distribute the workload evenly across the cluster without increasing the number of nodes.</p>\n<p>Which adjustment should the data engineering team make to achieve the desired distribution?</p>","answers":["<span>Use columns that are frequently used in <code>JOIN</code> conditions as the sort key.</span>","<span>Identify the largest dimension table and alter its distribution key to <code>EVEN</code>.</span>","Scale up the size of the node experiencing high CPU utilization.","<span>Identify the column that is most commonly referenced in the <code>WHERE</code> clause of <code>SELECT</code> statements. Then, assign this column as the new primary key</span>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><strong>Data distribution in Amazon Redshift</strong> refers to how data is stored across the nodes and slices within a cluster. When you load data into a Redshift table, the rows are distributed and stored across these slices according to the table's distribution style.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY distribution styles:</strong> The rows are distributed based on the values in a specified distribution key column. Rows with the same value are stored together. This helps optimize queries that filter or join on the distribution key column.</p>\n<p>The different key concepts in Amazon Redshift are:</p>\n<p><strong>Distribution key:</strong> This column is used to distribute the rows across nodes in the cluster. It is specified when creating a table using the <code>DISTKEY</code> clause.</p>\n<p><strong>Sort key:</strong> This column is used to order or sort the rows stored on each node. It is specified using the <code>SORTKEY</code> clause. Multiple columns can be used as sort keys.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary key:</strong> This uniquely identifies each row in the table. It consists of the distribution key as the partition key and can optionally include a sort key.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/bcaa621abee81fcb85f0c9e9705b8cc501cf4830f6b793a3b6ae014e0cc27f17.jpg\"></p>\n<p>The data engineering team should adjust the distribution key to correct the load imbalance across the cluster nodes. The aim is to achieve a more balanced distribution of queries and storage across all nodes to prevent any single node from becoming a bottleneck. A well-chosen <code>DISTKEY</code> ensures that the data is distributed uniformly across all slices of each node, which helps in achieving an even distribution of query load. If the current <code>DISTKEY</code> is leading to high CPU usage, it suggests that it is not evenly distributing the data due to its low cardinality or because it doesn't align well with the query patterns. Choosing a column with a more evenly distributed range of values for the <code>DISTKEY</code> can solve this issue.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong> Identify the largest dimension table and alter its distribution key to <code>EVEN</code>.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use columns that are frequently used in <code>JOIN</code> conditions as the sort key</strong></span> is incorrect. Sort keys optimize query performance by determining the order of data stored and are mainly used to improve the speed of range-restricted queries, not to balance CPU load across nodes. Therefore, adjusting sort keys would not address the issue of uneven distribution.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Scale up the size of the node experiencing high CPU utilization</strong></span> is incorrect because scaling up a single node is not possible in Redshift, as the cluster's nodes are uniform. Moreover, the aim is to balance the load without changing the node count or their configurations.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong> Identify the column that is most commonly referenced in the <code>WHERE</code> clause of <code>SELECT</code> statements. Then, assign this column as the new primary key</strong></span> is incorrect. This would only improve query performance, not necessarily solve the uneven distribution of workload across the cluster.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:<br>\n</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":17,"sourceNumber":25,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A biomedical company has various medical devices that send health metrics to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams.</p>\n<p>The company wants to analyze current versus historical data and display the insights on a dashboard in near real-time. To achieve this, a data engineer must set up a pipeline that stores incoming data streams in an Amazon Redshift Serverless data warehouse.</p>\n<p>Which solution will fulfill these requirements with LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Create an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream using Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon Redshift as the destination.","Stream the data to the Amazon Redshift database using Amazon Redshift Streaming Ingestion.","<span>Ingest the data to Amazon Redshift by first staging the data in Amazon S3 and using the <code>COPY</code> command.</span>","Ingest the data into an Amazon Aurora database and use the zero-ETL integration to sync it with the Amazon Redshift database."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams</strong> is a fully managed service that can continuously capture and store terabytes of data per hour from hundreds of thousands of sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon Redshift Streaming Ingestion</strong> allows ingesting streaming data from Kinesis data streams into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse in near real-time for analytics. It can ingest hundreds of megabytes of data per second with low latency.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c4bf798fd9f74674d11fae992ca8ff60da944a9dbec7c78abf6ee8bbc45c61c9.png\"></p>\n<p>By utilizing Amazon Redshift's Streaming Ingestion, the company can directly ingest data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams into an Amazon Redshift materialized view, bypassing the need for additional data processing layers or staging areas like S3 and directly ingesting data into Redshift. For the given scenario of streaming biomedical equipment data, the steps would be:</p>\n<p>Stream the data from biomedical equipment into an Amazon Kinesis data stream.</p>\n<p>Define an external schema in Redshift pointing to the Kinesis data stream using <code>CREATE EXTERNAL SCHEMA</code>.</p>\n<p>Create a materialized view to consume the streaming data from the external schema using <code>CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW</code>.</p>\n<p>Periodically refresh the materialized view using <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code> to load new data from the stream into Redshift tables.</p>\n<p>This enables near real-time analytics on the streaming biomedical data as it continuously gets ingested into Redshift with low latency using streaming ingestion. The data engineer can then run queries on both the incoming streaming data as well as historical data for analysis.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Stream the data to the Amazon Redshift database using Amazon Redshift Streaming Ingestion.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream using Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon Redshift as the destination</strong></span> is incorrect. While Kinesis Data Firehose supports data streaming into Amazon Redshift, it involves staging data in Amazon S3 before loading it into Redshift, which can introduce additional latency compared to direct streaming ingestion.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Ingest the data to Amazon Redshift by first staging the data in Amazon S3 and using the <code>COPY</code> command</strong></span> is incorrect. This method requires data to be first stored in S3, followed by a batch loading process using the COPY command. It's less efficient for real-time data processing needs due to the delay in batch processing.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Ingest the data into an Amazon Aurora database and use the zero-ETL integration to sync it with the Amazon Redshift database</strong></span> is incorrect. Although Amazon Aurora offers zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift, it is not directly related to streaming data ingestion from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Moreover, this approach leads to extra operational overhead from unnecessarily managing two databases.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Cheat Sheets for Amazon Kinesis and Amazon Redshift:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":18,"sourceNumber":26,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A retail company needs to improve SQL query efficiency in its data warehouse, which uses Amazon Redshift. It cannot expand the cluster due to cost constraints. The data engineering team reviews <code>STL_QUERY_METRICS</code> to monitor query performance and identify workloads that may benefit from improved table design.<br>\nThe company's data is in various tables, with some having thousands of megabytes of data, while others are smaller and updated infrequently.</p>\n<p>Which strategy is the most effective for meeting these requirements?</p>","answers":["Ensure that primary and foreign keys are defined for each table while maintaining an EVEN distribution style across all tables.","Change the distribution style of larger tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables.","Change the distribution style of small tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables.","Change the distribution style of small tables to KEY distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Data distribution in Amazon Redshift</strong> refers to how data is stored across the nodes and slices within a cluster. When you load data into a Redshift table, the rows are distributed and stored across these slices according to the table's distribution style.</p>\n<p>The different distribution styles in <strong>Amazon Redshift</strong> are:</p>\n<p><strong>EVEN distribution</strong> - The rows are distributed evenly across all nodes and slices in a round-robin fashion, regardless of the values in any particular column. This is used when the table doesn't participate in joins or when there is no clear choice of key distribution column.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY distribution</strong> - The rows are distributed based on the values in a specified distribution key column. Rows with the same value are stored together physically. This helps optimize queries that filter or join on the distribution key column.</p>\n<p><strong>ALL distribution</strong> - A copy of the entire table is stored on each node, so every row is collocated for any join query involving that table. However, it requires more storage and load time compared to EVEN or KEY. Used for relatively static tables.</p>\n<p><strong>AUTO distribution</strong> - Redshift automatically assigns the optimal distribution style based on table size and characteristics. It initially uses ALL and may later change to KEY or EVEN, depending on how the table grows.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5c45822348caa14c54f7cd339e3a2cdc7982da85c985cdfbc49890d6fb8275c0.jpg\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift DISTSTYLE ALL copying the full table across all compute nodes\"></p>\n<p>Considering the described scenario, the solution that best meets the requirement involves using the ALL distribution style for the small, infrequently updated table. Changing the smaller dimension tables to ALL distribution would replicate these tables to all nodes, allowing them to be colocated with larger fact tables during joins. Furthermore, setting the primary keys on dimension tables and referencing these as foreign keys in fact tables allows Redshift to leverage these constraints for query optimization.</p>\n<p>Using ALL distribution for relevant small tables improves query performance without requiring the company to expand its cluster due to cost constraints. Lastly, ALL distribution is well-suited for smaller tables that see infrequent changes, as it avoids data redistribution during updates.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Change the distribution style of small tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<strong> <span>Ensure that primary and foreign keys are defined for each table while maintaining an EVEN distribution style across all tables</span></strong> is incorrect because the EVEN distribution is just not optimal for small, infrequently updated tables that are frequently joined with large tables. It can lead to unnecessary data redistribution during joins, which can degrade performance.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Change the distribution style of larger tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables</span></strong> is incorrect. Distributing large tables with an ALL distribution style would significantly increase storage requirements and could slow down query performance due to the additional data being processed on each node.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Change the distribution style of small tables to KEY distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables</span></strong> is incorrect. While KEY distribution can be effective for distributing data based on the values of a specific column (usually a join key), using ALL for small tables is simply more efficient as it removes the need for redistribution during joins, leading to faster query execution.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Defining_constraints.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Defining_constraints.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":19,"sourceNumber":27,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A video streaming company is preparing a dataset to train a predictive model. The dataset contains null values, irrelevant data, and duplicates. The goal is to improve data quality so that the company can accurately classify subscribers into those likely to churn and those likely to stay.</p>\n<p>Which solution can meet the company's needs with LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Implement SageMaker Canvas for automatic data cleansing and organization. Configure a prediction model using multi-class text classification within SageMaker Canvas to label the data.","Utilize SageMaker HyperPod for data cleansing, then apply a SageMaker built-in algorithm for the creation of a binary classification model.","Implement SageMaker Canvas's automated data cleansing and preparation features for optimal data structuring. Configure to use the built-in binary classification model within SageMaker Canvas for prediction.","Deploy SageMaker Data Wrangler for data cleansing, then use SageMaker Autopilot to train a binary classification model."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon SageMaker</strong> is a service that simplifies building, training, and deploying machine learning models. SageMaker is a platform that facilitates the creation of high-quality machine learning models by abstracting away the complexity typically associated with each process step. It offers an integrated Jupyter Notebook environment for data exploration and preprocessing, optimized and ready-to-use algorithms, and a fully managed deployment service. This enables practitioners to focus more on solving the problem than on the operational overhead of machine learning pipelines.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2c2ab84f34aff5fe1406be235636e1aa4a1d4b3808d5496ad7d65729cefe88f3.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p><strong>SageMaker Canvas</strong> is a feature of Amazon SageMaker that represents a no-code approach to machine learning. It makes machine learning accessible to users of all technical backgrounds. With a user-friendly interface, it automates data preparation, model selection, training, and deployment. This allows users to create predictive models without needing to write any code. Canvas simplifies the entire process of applying machine learning, from data cleaning to making predictions.</p>\n<p>In building a model, SageMaker Canvas offers different types of models, which are the following:</p>\n<p><strong>Numeric prediction</strong> (<em>regression</em>) model is used to predict numeric data such as the price of houses based on features like square footage.</p>\n<p><strong>Categorical prediction</strong> (<em>classification</em>) model is mainly used in categorizing data into groups, which also have two categorical prediction model types:</p>\n<p><strong>2 category prediction</strong> (also known as <em>binary classification</em> in machine learning) is used when you have two categories you want to predict for your data, such as determining whether a customer will churn.</p>\n<p><strong>3+ category prediction</strong> (<em>multi-class classification</em>) is used to predict three or more categories, such as a customer's loan status based on past payments.</p>\n<p><strong>Time series forecasting</strong> is useful for making predictions over a period of time, such as sales predictions for the next quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Image prediction</strong> (<em>single-label image classification</em>) model can be used for image labeling.</p>\n<p><strong>Text prediction </strong>(<em>multi-class text classification</em>) If you have a collection of customer reviews for a particular product and you want to categorize the passages of text to determine whether customers had a positive, negative, or neutral opinion about the product, you can utilize this model to assign a label to each passage of text. This approach can help you analyze the sentiment of the reviews and gain valuable insights about the product's performance.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Implement SageMaker Canvas's automated data cleansing and preparation features for optimal data structuring. Configure to use the built-in binary classification model within SageMaker Canvas for prediction.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement SageMaker Canvas for automatic data cleansing and organization. Configure a prediction model using multi-class text classification within SageMaker Canvas to label the data</strong></span> is incorrect. Although SageMaker Canvas is useful for data cleaning and organization, the primary function of multi-class text classification is to analyze labeled text data and predict labels for unlabeled text passages. This doesn't align with the company's requirement to categorize subscribers into two groups.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize SageMaker HyperPod for data cleansing, then apply a SageMaker built-in algorithm for the creation of a binary classification model</strong></span> is incorrect. SageMaker HyperPod is only ideal for provisioning resilient clusters to run machine learning workloads and develop models like foundation models (FMs), large language models (LLMs), and diffusion models. Thus, it does not fulfill the company's requirement to build a predictive model that will forecast subscriber discontinuation.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Deploy SageMaker Data Wrangler for data cleansing, then use SageMaker Autopilot to train a binary classification model</strong></span> is incorrect. SageMaker Data Wrangler and SageMaker Autopilot are indeed powerful tools for data preparation and model training. While these tools offer extensive functionality, they also introduce more overhead in terms of setting up, configuration, and operation. This can increase the effort and time required to develop the predictive model, unlike SageMaker Canvas, which offers a more streamlined, no-code solution for users who may not have deep technical expertise in machine learning.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-prepare-data.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-prepare-data.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-build-model.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-build-model.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon SageMaker Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/</a></p>"},{"id":20,"sourceNumber":28,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer executes a weekly AWS Glue job, sourcing data from an Amazon S3 bucket. This bucket receives regular data uploads from a Lambda function, organized in a path format by year, month, and day.</p>\n<p>The data engineer must create a solution to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog as soon as new partitions are added to the bucket.</p>\n<p>Which solution will achieve this objective?</p>","answers":["Configure a scheduled AWS Glue crawler that runs every day.","<span>Set the <code>enableUpdateCatalog</code> option to <code>true</code> within the Glue job’s script.</span>","<span>Implement a code within the Lambda function to automatically invoke the Boto3 <code>create_partition</code> API in AWS Glue.</span>","<span>Run the <code>MSCK REPAIR TABLE</code> command directly from the Amazon Athena query editor after new data is added.</span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue</strong> is a managed ETL service that simplifies data preparation and analytics. The AWS Glue Data Catalog is a central repository for metadata regarding data utilized in ETL jobs within AWS Glue. This catalog is critical for managing the schema and structure of data in various storage mediums, including Amazon S3. Crawlers within AWS Glue are designed to scan data stores, identify data schema, and populate the Data Catalog with metadata tables describing the data, facilitating seamless ETL processes.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/04e81e7faba69a1d568f53c8e339759d82ebc90576560453ac89d3124ca4af09.png\"></p>\n<p>Boto3, AWS's SDK for Python, offers various methods to interact with AWS services programmatically, including AWS Glue. Through the Boto3 AWS Glue client, developers can directly invoke operations such as <code>create_partition</code> , allowing for real-time updates to the AWS Glue Data Catalog as soon as data is written to S3, without the need for manual intervention or waiting for scheduled crawlers.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong> Implement a code within the Lambda function to automatically invoke the Boto3 <code>create_partition</code> API in AWS Glue.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure a scheduled AWS Glue crawler that runs every day</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach involves updating the AWS Glue Data Catalog at scheduled intervals, which may not meet the real-time needs after an ETL job. Although AWS Glue crawlers can effectively discover new and existing data in S3 and create or update the Data Catalog, the once-a-week schedule may cause a delay in reflecting new data partitions. This delay can affect the availability of data for analysis and querying.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set the <code>enableUpdateCatalog</code> option to <code>true</code> within the Glue job’s script</strong></span> is incorrect. This option is only helpful if you want to update the Data Catalog with the output data from the job. It won't automatically detect and add new partitions from the input data source.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Run the <code>MSCK REPAIR TABLE</code> command directly from the Amazon Athena query editor after new data is added </strong></span>is incorrect. While this command can repair and add new partitions to the Data Catalog, it is not an automated solution. Running this command manually from the Athena query editor requires manual intervention and may miss new partitions if not executed promptly after data is added to the S3 bucket.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-catalog-partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-catalog-partitions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html#\">https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html#</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":21,"sourceNumber":29,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company needs to optimize the performance of an Amazon Redshift table containing two years of customer purchase data. Currently, the table uses an interleaved sort key on the <code>country_id</code> column. Due to weekly data additions, the distribution of data has become uneven, leading to slower queries and less available storage.</p>\n<p>Which <code>VACUUM</code> command will effectively reclaim disk space and improve query performance?</p>","answers":["<span><code>VACUUM DELETE ONLY</code></span>","<span><code>VACUUM FULL</code></span>","<span><code>VACUUM REINDEX</code></span>","<span><code>VACUUM SORT ONLY</code></span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>VACUUM</strong> is a command in Amazon Redshift that optimizes query performance by reclaiming disk space from deleted or updated rows, rebuilding indexes, and reorganizing tables. The main VACUUM commands are:</p>\n<p><strong><code>VACUUM FULL</code></strong> - Reclaims disk space, rebuilds indexes, and re-sorts all rows in the table. This is the default <code>VACUUM</code> operation in Redshift.</p>\n<p><strong><code>VACUUM DELETE ONLY</code></strong> - Only reclaims disk space occupied by deleted rows without re-sorting data. This is faster than VACUUM FULL but does not optimize query performance.</p>\n<p><strong><code>VACUUM REINDEX</code></strong> - Analyzes the distribution of values in interleaved sort key columns and performs a full VACUUM operation, including re-sorting. Used when interleaved sort keys are skewed.</p>\n<p><strong><code>VACUUM SORT ONLY</code></strong> - This command performs a sort-only operation on the table without reclaiming disk space. It is useful when rows are unsorted, but space is not an issue.</p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift automatically performs <strong><code>VACUUM DELETE ONLY</code></strong> operations in the background to reclaim space from deleted rows.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0f838a54cd3e98ca262afad60a080196b2d34a646e5c4723f77e3c280721c448.png\"></p>\n<p>The best VACUUM command for the company to use is <strong><code>VACUUM REINDEX</code></strong>. This command will re-sort the entire table based on the interleaved sort key, restoring optimal sort order and improving query performance by analyzing and rebalancing the distribution of values in the sort key columns. Since the sort key distribution is significantly uneven/skewed, using REINDEX instead of a standard VACUUM will be more effective. Additionally, VACUUM REINDEX will reclaim disk space from deleted or updated records fulfilling another of the scenario's requirements.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span><code>VACUUM REINDEX</code></span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong><code>VACUUM FULL</code></strong></span> is incorrect because, although it reclaims disk space and might improve overall table health, it does not specifically focus on the nuanced needs of interleaved sort key optimization, nor does it offer the most effective solution for analyzing sort keys in skewed data distributions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong><code>VACUUM DELETE ONLY</code></strong></span> is incorrect as it is primarily designed to free up space from deleted rows rather than focusing on optimizing sort keys or analyzing their order</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong><code>VACUUM SORT ONLY</code></strong></span> is incorrect. This command will simply re-sort rows within each block but won't necessarily reclaim disk space.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Reclaiming_storage_space202.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Reclaiming_storage_space202.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet: </strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":22,"sourceNumber":30,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p><span>A manufacturing company uses a multi-node Amazon Redshift cluster for its operational database and data warehousing. The company aims to streamline the process of updating its Redshift materialized views routinely through automation.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which solution requires the MINIMAL operational overhead to fulfill this requirement?</span></p>","answers":["Implement an AWS Step Function workflow to orchestrate the automation of materialized view updates.","Configure an AWS Glue workflow specifically to refresh the materialized views.","Launch an AWS Lambda user-defined function (UDF) to trigger updates within Redshift for the materialized views.","Execute scheduled refresh commands using the query editor v2 in Redshift."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Redshift&nbsp;</strong>is a managed data warehousing service that allows easy data analysis using SQL and existing BI tools. Amazon Redshift enables you to process massive amounts of structured data with advanced query optimization, columnar storage, and parallel processing, making it easy to run complex analytic queries.</p>\n<p>Additionally, Amazon Redshift service allows users to create and manage materialized views, which are stored or cached views of a query's result set for faster access. Redshift supports various automation options, including AWS services and external tools, to automate the updates of these views.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/986855c867f924b8dace526da6e1a5120541f7099623b94adcfb65e56ea5ec60.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift\"></p>\n<p>The query editor v2 in Amazon Redshift can execute SQL commands directly within the Redshift console, including commands to refresh materialized views. These commands can be scheduled to execute automatically and can be set up directly in the Redshift environment. This provides a straightforward method to automate materialized view updates without additional infrastructure or services, thereby minimizing operational overhead.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Execute scheduled refresh commands using the query editor v2 in Redshift.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Implement an AWS Step Function workflow to orchestrate the automation of materialized view updates&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. AWS Step Functions is a service that is primarily used for coordinating complex workflows involving multiple tasks and services, but it is not well-suited for running simple operations that occur entirely within Amazon Redshift. In this case, refreshing materialized views is a self-contained task within the Redshift environment, and using Step Functions would be an unnecessary overhead.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an AWS Glue workflow specifically to refresh the materialized views</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While Glue can connect to Redshift as a data source or target, using it solely for refreshing materialized views would require additional coding, job setup, and potentially moving data in and out of Redshift, which adds operational complexity.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Launch an AWS Lambda user-defined function (UDF) to trigger updates within Redshift for the materialized views</strong></span> is incorrect.&nbsp;Using AWS Lambda to refresh materialized views in Amazon Redshift adds complexity and typically requires custom code development and maintenance. It is less operationally efficient than Redshift's native scheduling capabilities.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-schedule-query.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-schedule-query.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon Redshift Overview:</strong></p>\n<p></p><div><iframe src=\"./📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo_files/jlLERNzhHOg.html\"></iframe></div><p></p>"},{"id":23,"sourceNumber":31,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer is building a pipeline in which an AWS Glue job first transforms data from a MySQL database and loads it into an S3 bucket. Following this, three Lambda functions process the data in parallel. The data engineer is looking for a way to orchestrate this workflow with the least amount of operational overhead.</p>\n<p>Which would fulfill these requirements?</p>","answers":["Create a state machine in AWS Step Functions to manage the execution sequence of the Glue job and Lambda functions.","Configure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon EC2. Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Lambda function and proceeds to the execution of the AWS Glue job.","Create an AWS Glue workflow that orchestrates the sequence of initiating the Glue job and triggering the Lambda functions.","Configure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS). Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Glue job followed by the execution of the Lambda functions."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Step Functions allows the coordination of multiple AWS services into serverless workflows. By designing a state machine, AWS Step Functions can manage the execution order, dependency tracking, and error handling of the tasks, which in this scenario include an AWS Lambda function and an AWS Glue job. This service provides a visual interface to monitor the steps of the workflow, significantly reducing the management overhead compared to managing infrastructure or writing custom orchestration code.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/cf9225b9bdb57314d0a5d9b030489e59b4ce29eb312dba0e80e326b805b97297.png\" alt=\"\">In this scenario, the data engineer can create task states in Step Functions for the Glue job and Lambda functions. Step Functions has AWS SDK integration, allowing direct Glue API calls to run a Glue job for data transformation and to invoke multiple Lambda functions for data processing. You can configure a parallel state to execute the Lambda functions independently of each other concurrently. This approach significantly reduces operational complexity and accelerates the overall process.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Create a state machine in AWS Step Functions to manage the execution sequence of the Glue job and Lambda functions.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon EC2. Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Lambda function and proceeds to the execution of the AWS Glue job </strong></span>is incorrect. This approach introduces significant operational overhead by requiring the management of Apache Airflow on EC2 instances, which is not necessary when using AWS Step Functions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Create an AWS Glue workflow that orchestrates the sequence of initiating the Glue job and triggering the Lambda functions </span></strong>is incorrect because Glue workflow is primarily designed for orchestrating Glue jobs and crawler schedules rather than coordinating external services like Lambda functions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS). Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Glue job followed by the execution of the Lambda functions</strong></span> is incorrect. Deploying Apache Airflow on Amazon EKS introduces complexity primarily associated with Kubernetes management. Although Amazon EKS can simplify the management of containerized applications, using it just for workflow orchestration of external services is an overkill. Therefore, this option does not satisfy the requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-multiple-etl-jobs-using-aws-step-functions-and-aws-lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-multiple-etl-jobs-using-aws-step-functions-and-aws-lambda/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/</a></p>"},{"id":24,"sourceNumber":32,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>An e-commerce company has observed new accounts being created with minor alterations in names and emails based on a list of flagged accounts. Their existing system cannot detect these subtle variations. The updated account records are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket for inspection.</p>\n<p>A data engineer must develop a solution for identifying accounts that could match a known suspicious account.</p>\n<p>Which solution would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["<span>Execute SQL queries via Amazon Athena using the <code>REGEXP_LIKE</code> function to search for keywords.  </span>","<span>Implement a <code>FindMatches</code> transform in AWS Glue to identify potentially fraudulent user accounts.</span>","Create an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use PySpark’s regular expression function to compare new accounts against the list of flagged accounts.","<span>Use Redshift Spectrum with the <code>SQL LIKE</code> operator to search for similarities in data.</span>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue FindMatches Machine Learning (ML) Transform enables you to identify duplicate or matching records in your dataset, even when the records do not have a common unique identifier and no fields match exactly. This will not require writing any code or knowing how machine learning works.<br>\n<img src=\"exams/assets/9e39481d3df05e7cb101d52cea586b5a2cd05ee51b80e01c63a7520e429efafe.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>FindMatches can be useful in many different problems, such as:</p>\n<p><strong>- Matching customers</strong>: Linking customer records across different customer databases, even when many customer fields do not match exactly across the databases (e.g., different name spelling, address differences, missing or inaccurate data, etc).</p>\n<p><strong>- Matching products</strong>: Matching products in your catalog against other product sources, such as product catalog against a competitor's catalog, where entries are structured differently.</p>\n<p><strong>- Improving fraud detection</strong>: Identifying duplicate customer accounts, determining when a newly created account is (or might be) a match for a previously known fraudulent user.</p>\n<p><strong>- Other matching problems</strong>: Match addresses, movies, parts lists, etc. In general, if a human being could look at your database rows and determine that they were a match, there is a really good chance that the FindMatches transform can help you.</p>\n<p>In the scenario where there's a need to detect subtle variations in names and emails from flagged data, using AWS Glue FindMatches Transform offers a direct solution.&nbsp;This would allow the company to spot discrepancies without the intricacies of setting up custom models or understanding how Machine Learning works. Custom machine learning can be expensive and tricky. With FindMatches, you avoid the time and cost of setting up and tuning your own model.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Implement a <code>FindMatches</code> transform in AWS Glue to identify potentially fraudulent user accounts. </strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Execute SQL queries via Amazon Athena using the <code>REGEXP_LIKE</code> function to search for keywords</strong></span> is incorrect. While <code>REGEXP_LIKE</code> allows for pattern matching, it requires precise regex patterns to be effective. This approach could miss variations that don't fit the predefined patterns and might not scale well for complex variations without extensive manual effort to update and maintain these patterns.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use PySpark’s regular expression function to compare new accounts against the list of flagged accounts </strong></span>is incorrect. Regular expressions work for pattern matching but might not be the best tool for catching subtle variations in data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use Redshift Spectrum with the <code>SQL LIKE</code> operator to search for similarities in data </span></strong>is incorrect. The <code>LIKE</code> operator works on patterns and is typically used for exact substrings or basic wildcard matching. When it comes to variations in names and emails, a pattern might not always capture all discrepancies. Thus, this approach might miss subtle variations and generate false negatives.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/03/aws-glue-fuzzy-findmatches/\">https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/03/aws-glue-fuzzy-findmatches/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/matching-patient-records-with-the-aws-lake-formation-findmatches-transform/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/matching-patient-records-with-the-aws-lake-formation-findmatches-transform/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":25,"sourceNumber":34,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer is developing an application that involves sending data for transformation to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. Multiple Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) tasks will poll and consume messages from the queue. The engineer must ensure that each message is processed once before being deleted.</p>\n<p>Which events can lead to the removal of messages from an SQS queue? (Select THREE.)</p>","answers":["<span>A <code>DeleteMessage</code> API call was made to Amazon SQS.</span>","<span>The <code>maxReceiveCount</code> for a message was exceeded.</span>","A purge operation was performed on the queue.","An application receives a message without deleting it.","The visibility timeout expires on a message.","The configuration for a queue is edited."],"correct":[0,1,2],"explanation":"<p>In Amazon SQS, several events can lead to the removal of messages, which is crucial for ensuring the queue's efficiency and reliability. A DeleteMessage API call is a direct method to remove a message from the queue, typically after it has been processed by a consumer. This ensures that messages are not processed more than necessary. Reaching the maxReceiveCount for a message is another way messages are removed; this occurs when a message has been received a specified number of times but not deleted, indicating processing issues and usually results in the message being sent to a dead-letter queue. Furthermore, performing a purge operation on the queue instantly clears all messages, useful for resetting or troubleshooting the queue.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/34ece88cf927a9f2cb5943bf4f08cc447640540200b78240c3cd13e4b6d79282.jpg\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- A <code>DeleteMessage</code> API call was made to Amazon SQS.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- The <code>maxReceiveCount</code> for a message was exceeded.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- A purge operation was performed on the queue.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>An application receives a message without deleting</span> <span>it</span> </strong>is incorrect. Merely receiving a message does not remove it from the queue. Instead, it triggers a visibility timeout, during which the message is not visible to other consumers but remains in the queue.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The visibility timeout expires on a message</strong></span> is incorrect. The expiry of the visibility timeout makes the message available to other consumers again but does not remove it from the queue.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The configuration for a queue</strong> <strong>is edited</strong></span> is incorrect. Editing a queue's configuration can change its behavior and settings but does not directly lead to message removal from the queue.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Resources</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-using-purge-queue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-using-purge-queue.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/</a></p>"},{"id":26,"sourceNumber":35,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A marketing agency runs daily sales campaigns and stores the results in their Amazon S3 data lake. They want to implement a new platform that allows analysts to visually verify data quality against set standards before any transformation or analysis.</p>\n<p>Which set of actions can fulfill the requirements with the least amount of management overhead?</p>","answers":["Implement AWS Kinesis Data Firehose for streaming S3 data lake contents to an Amazon OpenSearch cluster. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for visualizing data quality metrics.","Connect the S3 data lake as a data set in AWS Glue DataBrew and create a profiling job for evaluating data quality metrics.","Develop AWS Glue ETL jobs using the Deequ Spark library for daily data validation. Store ETL outputs in S3 and instruct analysts to use Amazon Athena for querying and visualizing data quality metrics.","Set up an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark and Apache Zeppelin. Conduct a daily transfer of S3 data to the cluster's Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Use Spark MLlib for quality checks and Zeppelin notebooks for result visualization."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that helps data analysts with data preparation tasks such as data profiling, cleaning, and normalizing all without the need to write any code.</p>\n<p>You can create your own data quality rules to validate business requirements for specific datasets. These rules enable you to check for issues such as duplicate values in columns or to ensure uniqueness between columns. You can manage these rules by grouping them into rule sets and applying them during profile jobs. The system displays the results on a dashboard, offering a clear and concise view of how well the data complies with the set business rules.<br>\n<img src=\"exams/assets/7524687b59974ce9a6a082a22426eef0ef3c6fabdb26b4bfed61a98ee685d2ad.jpg\" alt=\"\">Since AWS Glue DataBrew is serverless, you do not need to provision or manage any infrastructure to use it. With AWS Glue DataBrew, you can easily and quickly create and execute data preparation recipes through a visual interface. The service automatically scales up or down based on the size and complexity of your data, so you only pay for the resources you use.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Connect the S3 data lake as a data set in AWS Glue DataBrew and create a profiling job for evaluating data quality metrics.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement AWS Kinesis Data Firehose for streaming S3 data lake contents to an Amazon OpenSearch cluster. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for visualizing data quality metrics</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This approach involves the unnecessary complexity of streaming data from S3 using Kinesis Data Firehose when the data is already stored in the S3 data lake. Moreover, OpenSearch is more oriented towards real-time data streaming and visualization, making it an overkill solution for the scenario.</p>\n<p>The option says:<span><strong>&nbsp;Develop AWS Glue ETL jobs using the Deequ Spark library for daily data validation. Store ETL outputs in S3 and instruct analysts to use Amazon Athena for querying and visualizing data quality metrics</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;Deequ is an open-source library built on top of Apache Spark to verify data quality at scale.&nbsp;While it excels at unit testing data for quality constraints, it's not primarily tailored for visual data profiling.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark and Apache Zeppelin. Conduct a daily transfer of S3 data to the cluster's Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Use Spark MLlib for quality checks and Zeppelin notebooks for result visualization&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Deploying and managing an Amazon EMR cluster typically involves more administrative overhead. Additionally, using Spark's MLlib for data quality assessments in this scenario introduces complexity that may be unnecessary, as the requirement is for visual data profiling and not machine learning.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":27,"sourceNumber":36,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company has a data warehouse solution running in an Amazon Redshift cluster in AWS. The company’s data engineer runs the following query in Redshift:</p>\n<p><code>select current_schema(), userid from sales_data where order_date = '2025-01-01' limit 1;</code></p>\n<p>Upon running the query, there was an error in the output that says:</p>\n<p><code>INFO:  Function \"current_schema()\" not supported</code><br>\n<code>ERROR:  Specified types or functions (one per INFO message) not supported on Redshift tables.</code></p>\n<p>What is the MOST likely root cause of this issue?</p>","answers":["<span>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can't be run with a query that references a Redshift <code>sales_data</code> user table that resides on the leader nodes.</span>","<span>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can't be run with a query that references a Redshift <code>sales_data</code> user table that resides on the compute nodes.</span>","<span>The<code>current_schema()</code> is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can't be run with a query that references a Redshift <code>user_id</code> column that resides on the compute nodes.</span>","<span>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can't be run because it does not reference a table.</span>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift has two types of nodes: <strong>leader nodes</strong> and <strong>compute nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Compute Nodes</strong>: These nodes store user-created tables and process queries. They handle data storage, filtering, and computations.</p>\n<p><strong>Leader Node</strong>:&nbsp;This node manages query execution, distributes work to compute nodes, and stores system tables. It also manages external communication and aggregates the results from the compute nodes. Certain functions, including&nbsp;<code>current_schema()</code>, are <strong>leader node–only functions</strong>, meaning they can only be executed on the leader node.</p>\n<p>This node manages query execution, distributes work to compute nodes, and stores system tables.</p>\n<p><strong>System Tables</strong>: Redshift has several system tables that store metadata and information about the cluster, databases, schemas, tables, and users. These tables are typically prefixed with \"PG_\" or \"STV_\".</p>\n<p><strong>User-Defined Tables</strong>: These are the tables created by users to store their data in Redshift.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/798db4aabb6a36bc73703b758eb7a9a367f50614ab0d82065335421d6823f434.png\" alt=\"Redshift Compute Leader Node\"></p>\n<p>The sales_data table in the query is a user-created table, which resides on the compute nodes. Redshift does not allow leader node–only functions, like current_schema(), to be executed in queries that reference user-created tables on the compute nodes, as this would require the function to run on compute nodes, which is not supported.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the error occurs because the current_schema() function, which can only run on the leader node, is used in a query that references the sales_data user table, which is stored on the compute nodes.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can't be run with a query that references a Redshift <code>sales_data</code> user table that resides on the compute nodes.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can't be run with a query that references a Redshift <code>sales_data</code> user table that resides on the leader nodes</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because the <strong><code></code></strong><code>current_schema</code> is actually a leader-only function and not for compute node-only.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can't be run with a query that references a Redshift <code>user_id</code> column that resides on the compute nodes</strong></span> is incorrect. Similar to the previous option, the <strong><code></code></strong><code>current_schema</code> is actually a leader-only function and not for compute node-only. Additionally, the error message in the question refers primarily to the <code>sales_data</code> table, not a <code>user_id</code> table.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The <code>current_schema()</code> is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can't be run because it does not reference a table</strong></span> is incorrect simply because the issue is not due to the absence of a table reference but because of the leader node function being used with a table from the compute nodes.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_SQL_functions_leader_node_only.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_SQL_functions_leader_node_only.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_sql-functions-leader-node.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_sql-functions-leader-node.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":28,"sourceNumber":39,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A marketing analyst is utilizing Amazon Athena to analyze campaign performance data hosted on an S3 bucket. The analyst has created an external table in Athena to aggregate the data, containing metrics like campaign names, impressions, clicks, and orders. The aim is to pinpoint which marketing campaigns yielded the highest order counts and filter out any campaigns that generated less than 100 orders.</p>\n<p>To achieve this objective, the analyst drafts the following initial query:<br>\n<code><br>\nSELECT campaign, SUM(orders) AS total_orders<br>\nFROM campaign_performance<br>\nGROUP BY campaign<br>\n</code></p>\n<p>The analyst must ensure that only the top-performing campaigns are included in the final report.</p>\n<p>Which adjustment should the analyst make to the query?</p>","answers":["<span>Append <code>HAVING SUM(clicks) &gt; 100</code> following the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause.</span>","<span>Include a <code>WHERE orders &gt; 100</code> condition before the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause.</span>","<span>Insert <code>LIMIT 100</code> at the end of the query to limit the number of results.</span>","<span>Append <code>HAVING SUM(orders) &gt; 100</code> followed by <code>ORDER BY total_orders DESC;</code> after the GROUP BY clause.</span>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Data distribution in Amazon Athena</strong> pertains to the organization of data stored in Amazon S3 when running interactive queries using standard SQL. Unlike traditional databases, Athena allows for the analysis of vast datasets without the need for database loading or server maintenance.</p>\n<p>The <code>HAVING</code> clause, when used in conjunction with the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause allows for the filtering of groups based on specific conditions applied to aggregate functions like <code>SUM</code>, <code>COUNT</code>, <code>AVG</code>, etc. This clause is applied after groups are formed, enabling the filtering of groups based on aggregated values. It's important to note that any column referenced in the <code>HAVING</code> clause must be part of the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause or an aggregate function.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/80ba254e0af5a81084c71e39a703b7eb2a9e92a18be33961a253ec76690b92a2.png\"></p>\n<p>The correct strategy for refining the query to identify top-performing campaigns involves using the <code>HAVING</code> clause to filter grouped results based on the aggregate function <code>SUM(orders)</code>. The addition of <code>ORDER BY total_orders DESC;</code> ensures the presentation of results in descending order of total orders, facilitating a quick assessment of the highest-performing campaigns.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Append <code>HAVING SUM(orders) &gt; 100</code> followed by <code>ORDER BY total_orders DESC;</code> after the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Append <code>HAVING SUM(clicks) &gt; 100</code> following the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause</strong></span> is incorrect as it filters campaigns based on the number of clicks instead of orders, which does not align with the goal of measuring campaign success by orders generated.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Include a <code>WHERE orders &gt; 100</code> condition before the <code>GROUP BY</code> clause</strong></span> is incorrect because the <code>WHERE</code> clause filters individual rows before aggregation, which is not suitable when filtering based on the sum of orders per campaign.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Insert <code>LIMIT 100</code> at the end of the query to limit the number of results</strong></span> is incorrect as this approach just limits the number of campaigns returned rather than filtering campaigns based on the sum of orders generated.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/select.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/select.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/clean-rooms/latest/sql-reference/r_HAVING_clause.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/clean-rooms/latest/sql-reference/r_HAVING_clause.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:<br>\n</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>"},{"id":29,"sourceNumber":41,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company gathers a large amount of data from user interactions via its user registration system, which is then stored in an Amazon S3 data lake. Some of this data contains personally identifiable information (PII). The company wants to automate the process of identifying new and existing data that contains sensitive personal information and mask it before any analysis.</p>\n<p>The data engineer has been tasked to redact the PII automatically through a masking S3 Object Lambda function that’s powered by a prebuilt <code>ComprehendPiiRedactionS3ObjectLambda</code> Lambda function. In addition, the solution must also be capable of automatically triggering a notification when a piece of personally identifiable information is detected from the data lake and provide a detailed report on the identified data.</p>\n<p>Which solution effectively meets these requirements with MINIMAL operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Activate Amazon Inspector to regularly assess the S3 data lake for compliance with data protection standards, including PII detection. Use Amazon EventBridge to trigger the masking function based on Inspector findings.","Implement S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function that analyzes objects for personally identifiable information (PII) using custom scripts. Upon detecting PII, the Lambda function then invokes the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function","Activate Amazon Macie within the AWS account for continuous monitoring and identification of personally identifiable information (PII) within the S3 data lake. Configure the Amazon EventBridge rule to capture Macie's findings and trigger the masking function as the designated target for this rule.","Utilize AWS Lake Formation to set up data access rules that automatically scan for personally identifiable information (PII). Trigger an AWS Lambda function to invoke the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function upon detection of sensitive data."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Macie</strong> is a security service that utilizes machine learning algorithms to automatically identify, classify, and secure sensitive data in AWS. Amazon Macie protects sensitive data, such as personally identifiable information (PII) (e.g., names, addresses, credit card numbers, and intellectual property), from unauthorized access. After identifying sensitive data, Macie generates detailed reports and alerts, which can be integrated with Amazon EventBridge for automated response actions.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/e5c55e99cad624971c9c1321fbaf667331f118c7d79ba4caee2609f3664194b0.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Using Amazon Macie in conjunction with Amazon EventBridge allows for the automation of the process to detect PII and trigger the masking application with minimal operational overhead. Macie's continuous monitoring capability ensures both new and existing data in the S3 data lake are analyzed for PII, aligning perfectly with the corporation's requirements for automating the identification and masking of personal information before analysis. Furthermore, the integration of Amazon Macie for PII detection and Amazon EventBridge for event-driven triggering of the masking application provides a seamless and automated workflow. This minimizes operational overhead while ensuring sensitive data is automatically masked, adhering to data protection standards.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Activate Amazon Macie within the AWS account for continuous monitoring and identification of personally identifiable information (PII) within the S3 data lake. Configure the Amazon EventBridge rule to capture Macie's findings and trigger the masking function as the designated target for this rule.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Activate Amazon Inspector to regularly assess the S3 data lake for compliance with data protection standards, including PII detection. Use Amazon EventBridge to trigger the masking application based on Inspector findings&nbsp;</span></strong>is incorrect because Amazon Inspector is primarily used for security assessments and vulnerability management of EC2 instances and applications, not for detecting PII in S3 objects.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Implement S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function that analyzes objects for personally identifiable information (PII) using custom scripts. Upon detecting PII, the Lambda function then invokes the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. This option&nbsp;involves significant operational overhead to develop, maintain, and scale custom scripts for&nbsp;personally identifiable information (PII) detection.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Utilize AWS Lake Formation to set up data access rules that automatically scan for personally identifiable information (PII). Trigger an AWS Lambda function to invoke the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function upon detection of sensitive data</span></strong> is incorrect. AWS Lake Formation is primarily used for managing secure data lake access and does not inherently provide automated PII detection capabilities like&nbsp;Amazon Macie.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventBridge.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventBridge.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-types.html#findings-sensitive-data-types\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-types.html#findings-sensitive-data-types</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-publish-event-schemas.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-publish-event-schemas.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Macie Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-macie/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-macie/</a></p>"},{"id":30,"sourceNumber":42,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company runs a complex analytics workload every week on an Amazon Redshift cluster. The workload includes the following tasks:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Launching a new Redshift cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Performing processing and loading data to an Amazon S3 bucket.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Terminating the Redshift cluster.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The company wants to minimize operational overhead and ensure that all these tasks are handled automatically. The solution must also be able to handle the complexity of the analytics functions.</p>\n<p>Which solution offers the LEAST operational overhead while meeting these requirements?</p>","answers":["Run Apache Oozie on Amazon EC2 to process the analytics workload.","Use Amazon Athena to process the analytics workload.","Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the analytics workload.","Use Amazon Redshift Serverless to run the analytics workload."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift Serverless is a fully managed data warehouse service that automatically scales compute resources up or down to meet the demands of your workload. This means that it can handle the processing of large amounts of data without requiring manual intervention to adjust compute capacity. This automatic scaling and task management significantly reduce operational overhead, making it an ideal solution for the company's needs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/e2f5fb13caf8e60c45008ee4727f18ec37ff50885420098241fe09f4cae49a32.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift Serverless\"></p>\n<p>Furthermore, Amazon Redshift Serverless is designed to handle complex analytics functions. It uses machine learning to deliver consistently fast performance, even for complex queries and large datasets. This means that it can efficiently manage the company’s complex analytics workload.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong>&nbsp;Use Amazon Redshift Serverless to run the analytics workload.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Run Apache Oozie on Amazon EC2 to process the analytics workload</strong></span> is incorrect because Apache Oozie is a workflow scheduler primarily designed for Hadoop ecosystems. Running Oozie on EC2 requires complex setup, including infrastructure provisioning, configuration of dependencies, and ongoing instance management. This results in high operational overhead, which does not meet the company's goal to reduce manual operations.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon Athena to process the analytics workload</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;While Athena is serverless and has low operational overhead, its use of pooled resources can lead to variable performance, especially for complex analytic workloads that demand consistent and high-performance computing resources.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the analytics workload</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Redshift Spectrum is simply an extension of a Redshift cluster that allows you to directly query data on S3. Amazon Redshift Spectrum still needs an existing Redshift cluster to function and does not operate on its own.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/\">https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/new-user-serverless.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/new-user-serverless.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-redshift-serverless-run-analytics-at-any-scale-without-having-to-manage-infrastructure/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-redshift-serverless-run-analytics-at-any-scale-without-having-to-manage-infrastructure/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":31,"sourceNumber":45,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer at a healthcare analytics firm is tasked with enhancing patient outcome predictions using a machine learning (ML) model. The model integrates diverse datasets, including patient records, treatment histories, and clinical trial data. As the model is set to be deployed in a production environment, the engineer must guarantee the integrity and reliability of the data for ML-driven predictions.</p>\n<p>Additionally, the engineer needs to streamline the data preparation process for the model's deployment. Although the team currently uses AWS Glue DynamicFrame for data transformation, a more robust approach is required to track data lineage and ensure data integrity.</p>\n<p>Which solution will effectively meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["Utilize Amazon Redshift to aggregate and analyze the data, ensuring quality and completeness before feeding it into the model.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker workflows, incorporating a SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking step for data preparation.","Run Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to carry out exploratory data analysis (EDA) and stage the prepared data.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Processing to process the input data and output to Amazon S3."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking offers a way to create and store information about the various steps in an ML workflow. This feature is integral for establishing model governance and audit standards, which are essential for ensuring the accuracy, completeness, and trustworthiness of the data used in ML decisions.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/9429bef7a95175cfd8cc1a4b8c0fe3072a302384ddb3e256cb0aa59cd6e14281.jpg\" alt=\"SageMaker Pipelines execution graph for an Abalone machine learning workflow\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon SageMaker workflows, incorporating a SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking step for data preparation.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon Redshift to aggregate and analyze the data, ensuring quality and completeness before feeding it into the model</strong></span> is incorrect. While Amazon Redshift is simply a powerful data warehousing service that provides capabilities for data aggregation and analysis, it does not directly address the specific need to automate data preparation or ensure data lineage tracking for ML model deployments.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Run Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to carry out exploratory data analysis (EDA) and stage the prepared data</strong></span> is incorrect. SageMaker Data Wrangler is primarily effective for conducting EDA and preparing data for ML models, but it does not inherently ensure the data's accuracy, completeness, and trustworthiness for ML decisions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement Amazon SageMaker Processing to process the input data and output to Amazon S3</strong></span> is incorrect. Amazon SageMaker Processing is useful for various data operations, including processing and validation, but it does not inherently provide assurances about the data's accuracy, completeness, and trustworthiness for ML decisions.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/lineage-tracking.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/lineage-tracking.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/pipelines-lineage-tracking.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/pipelines-lineage-tracking.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/</a></p>"},{"id":32,"sourceNumber":46,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A game retail company manages user purchase data in a MySQL database on Amazon RDS. They frequently query and analyze the most recent three months of data, amounting to several terabytes. The company also needs to store older data outside the database, yet access it for quarterly trend reports, which involves joining it with recent data.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options will provide optimal performance and a cost-effective solution based on the requirements?</p>","answers":["Synchronize a year’s worth of data on an Amazon RDS read replica. Move the older historical data to an Amazon S3 bucket for long-term storage. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data for report generation.","Use AWS Glue to perform ETL and incrementally load a year’s worth of data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. Run the regular queries against this cluster. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data to generate the reports.","Migrate historical data to Amazon S3. Set up a daily transfer of current data from Amazon RDS to Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift for regular queries and Redshift Spectrum to join the historical data in S3 with current data.","Configure an Amazon Aurora database to handle real-time data. Archive older data to an S3 bucket. Utilize Amazon QuickSight for analytics, combining current data in Aurora with historical data for comprehensive reporting."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Redshift Spectrum</strong> stands out as a highly efficient solution for querying data across Amazon S3 and Amazon Redshift. It leverages the benefits of low-cost storage and open data formats, along with Redshift's advanced query optimization and fast access to local disks. Redshift Spectrum's serverless architecture aligns with Amazon Athena's, negating the need for resource provisioning and management.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"></p>\n<p>In this scenario, two distinct query requirements are identified:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Regular analytics on the most recent three months of data.</li>\n<li>Periodic queries for quarterly reports, encompassing both historical and recent data.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To meet the first requirement, a regular transfer of current data from the MySQL database to Amazon Redshift is established, taking advantage of Redshift's powerful analytics capabilities. For the second requirement, given the infrequent access to historical data, storing it in S3 and querying via Redshift Spectrum is a more cost-effective approach than keeping it within Redshift. This method avoids data migration costs and utilizes Redshift's allocated resources for optimized query performance.</p>\n<p>Thus, the optimal solution is: <span><strong>Migrate historical data to Amazon S3. Set up a daily transfer of current data from Amazon RDS to Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift for regular queries and Redshift Spectrum to join the historical data in S3 with current data.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Synchronize a year’s worth of data on an Amazon RDS read replica. Move the older historical data to an Amazon S3 bucket for long-term storage. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data for report generation</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach is inefficient as it requires maintaining a full year's worth of data on an RDS read replica, which can be costly and doesn't leverage the optimized analytical capabilities of a dedicated analytics service like Amazon Redshift. Additionally, while Amazon Athena is effective for querying S3 data, it necessitates data movement between RDS and S3, which can be time-consuming and adds complexity.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue to perform ETL and incrementally load a year’s worth of data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. Run the regular queries against this cluster. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data to generate the reports</strong></span> is incorrect. In this method, there is an unnecessary duplication of effort and resources. Loading recent data into a Redshift cluster and then moving it again to S3 for Athena queries is inefficient. Redshift Spectrum would be a more streamlined and cost-effective solution since it can query data directly in S3 without the need for additional data movement.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an Amazon Aurora database to handle real-time data. Archive older data to an S3 bucket. Utilize Amazon QuickSight for analytics, combining current data in Aurora with historical data for comprehensive reporting</strong></span> is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora is a powerful database for transactional workloads, it is not optimized for the kind of large-scale analytical processing described in the scenario. Moreover, using Amazon QuickSight for analytics across Aurora and S3 introduces complexity in managing data across different storage solutions and may not offer the same level of performance as Redshift, especially for large-scale data joining and analytical queries.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References: </strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-getting-started-using-spectrum.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-getting-started-using-spectrum.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon Redshift Overview:</strong></p>\n<p></p><div><iframe src=\"./📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo_files/jlLERNzhHOg(1).html\"></iframe></div><p></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>"},{"id":33,"sourceNumber":47,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A financial company has an Amazon S3 bucket that is used as a data lake for various cryptocurrency data sent from crypto exchanges across the globe. The DevOps team notices inconsistencies in data upload times across different regions.</p>\n<p>The company aims to guarantee high availability and fast access to the S3 bucket data across all regions as well as ensure data sovereignty. The challenge lies in accomplishing these objectives with minimal management overhead.</p>\n<p>Which set of actions can fulfill the requirements with the LEAST amount of management overhead?</p>","answers":["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region.","Use Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region.","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS.","Use Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration</strong>&nbsp;is a bucket-level feature that enables fast, easy, and secure transfers of files over long distances between your client and your Amazon S3 bucket. It takes advantage of Amazon CloudFront’s globally distributed edge locations. As the data arrives at an edge location, data is routed to Amazon S3 over an optimized network path. This results in faster data transfers and reduced latency, which is crucial for the scenario’s requirement of fast access retrieval times.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/634c54b34080e185b6978e899070b3d3ac6188739d636e42d5bf3caa6fcc4da8.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p><strong>Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR)</strong>&nbsp;is a feature that automatically replicates data between buckets across different AWS Regions. This helps to reduce latency by allowing users to access a copy of the data that is closer to them. It also addresses the requirement of data sovereignty, as data is stored within the specific regions where the company operates.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0024cfca85c0bebc231b10f4105fe54cec8494b1ddb324b8d1b722b0b4911a2b.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Both S3 Transfer Acceleration and S3 Cross-Region Replication are fully managed services, which means that AWS handles all the heavy lifting, and you don’t have to worry about the operational burden of scaling, patching, and maintaining servers or software. This allows you to focus on your core business and applications, thereby fulfilling the requirement of the least amount of management overhead.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region</strong></span><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. Amazon S3 Express One Zone is a high-performance, single-Availability Zone storage class that's purpose-built to deliver consistent single-digit millisecond data access for one specific Availability Zone only. This type of storage class won't suit the scenario since the data sources are sending data from all over the globe. While S3 CRR can help with data sovereignty and availability, the use of S3 Express One Zone does not meet the requirement for fast transfer times from a group of global sources.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS</strong></span><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because while S3 Transfer Acceleration can improve access times, AWS Direct Connect does not address the requirement of data sovereignty as it does not replicate the data to different regions. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated network connection from your premises to AWS, but it does not automatically replicate data across regions.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS</strong></span> is incorrect because<strong>&nbsp;</strong>an Amazon S3 Express One Zone is not suitable for a solution where the data producers are sending files from different parts of the world. Take note that this particular storage class is only fast in a given Availability Zone and AWS Direct Connect does not address the requirement of data sovereignty as it does not replicate the data to different regions.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/transfer-acceleration/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/transfer-acceleration/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://medium.com/cloud-techies/s3-same-region-replication-srr-and-cross-region-replication-crr-34d446806bab\">https://medium.com/cloud-techies/s3-same-region-replication-srr-and-cross-region-replication-crr-34d446806bab</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>"},{"id":34,"sourceNumber":48,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p><span>An e-commerce company has implemented real-time inventory management using a DynamoDB table with multiple data partitions. This solution updates inventory levels as customers place orders on the website. However, during peak shopping hours, the data engineer has noticed an increase in throttled items in the DynamoDB table, which is degrading the system's performance. This causes delays in inventory updates.</span></p>\n<p><span>To address these problems, the company needs to:</span></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><span>Continuously monitor and diagnose the DynamoDB table's access patterns.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span>Analyze traffic trends during different times of the day.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span>Identify the most frequently accessed partition keys.</span></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span>What steps should the data engineer take to address these issues? (Select TWO.)</span></p>","answers":["Use Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights to find the most accessed and throttled items in the DynamoDB table.","Redesign the DynamoDB table to use a high cardinality key to distribute the read and write operations as evenly as possible.","Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the access patterns of the DynamoDB table.","Use Amazon Athena to analyze the access patterns of the DynamoDB table.","Increase the provisioned read and write capacity units of the DynamoDB table."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights is a diagnostic tool that provides a summarized view of your DynamoDB table’s traffic trends. It helps you identify the most frequently accessed partition keys, which can be useful in diagnosing throttling issues. By identifying the most accessed and throttled items, the data engineer can gain insights into the table's access patterns and take appropriate actions to mitigate the throttling issues.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4a0aa4e7ab08034fa033a6565d63a0099ffcc81e7fc474818fe567541fd7b85b.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>In DynamoDB, data is distributed across multiple partitions based on the partition key. If a few partition keys receive a majority of the read and write traffic, this can lead to hot partitions, where these partitions receive a disproportionately high volume of traffic. This can result in throttling issues. By redesigning the table to use a high cardinality key, the read and write operations can be distributed more evenly across all the partitions, reducing the likelihood of hot partitions and throttling.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Use Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights to find the most accessed and throttled items in the DynamoDB table.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Redesign the DynamoDB table to use a high cardinality key to distribute the read and write operations as evenly as possible.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the access patterns of the DynamoDB table</span></strong> is incorrect. While Amazon QuickSight is a powerful visualization tool, it does not provide the real-time diagnostic capabilities needed to identify and address throttling issues in a DynamoDB table. Its primary function is to provide a visual representation of your data rather than real-time diagnostics or performance optimization.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon Athena to analyze the access patterns of the DynamoDB table</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon Athena is just a query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 and other data sources using standard SQL. It’s not designed to analyze access patterns of a DynamoDB table.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Increase the provisioned read and write capacity units of the DynamoDB table</strong></span> is incorrect because while this might help in some cases, it’s not a guaranteed solution. If the workload is unevenly distributed across the partitions (due to a low cardinality key), increasing capacity may only lead to higher costs if it's not properly scaled based on the actual traffic patterns.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/contributorinsights_HowItWorks.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/contributorinsights_HowItWorks.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choosing-the-right-dynamodb-partition-key/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choosing-the-right-dynamodb-partition-key/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TroubleshootingThrottling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TroubleshootingThrottling.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon DynamoDB and Amazon CloudWatch Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/</a></p>"},{"id":35,"sourceNumber":52,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>An enterprise exports JSON and CSV files from a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) application to an Amazon S3 bucket. Additionally, they have various internal services that store data in Amazon RDS for MySQL, SQL Server, and Amazon DynamoDB.</p>\n<p>The data engineering division must create a system that allows data scientists to execute queries across all these data storage options using an SQL-like syntax.</p>\n<p>Which solution will satisfy these prerequisites while minimizing operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Use AWS Glue to crawl the data sources and store the metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. With Amazon Athena, use SQL and PartiQL to query structured and semi-structured data respectively.","Utilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, saving metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Run SQL queries using Redshift spectrum.","Utilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, with metadata stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Transform JSON format data to Apache Parquet or .csv using AWS Glue jobs, storing transformed data in S3. Query both original and transformed data using Amazon Athena.","Configure Amazon RDS to directly query data using federated queries for RDS, DynamoDB, and S3. Store JSON and .csv data in S3 and use SQL Server-linked servers for cross-database queries."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue crawlers are designed to discover and catalog data from various data sources automatically, creating and updating tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This catalog serves as a central metadata repository, which data engineers and scientists can use to understand the structure and schema of their data across multiple data stores. The process of using crawlers simplifies the discovery of data, as it automatically infers the schema of the data and categorizes it accordingly, which is crucial for efficiently managing and querying large datasets.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4e1bf2d783e751dd83f24ae392a24b61137276c045b8edd15874638912af7764.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>By leveraging Amazon Athena, which supports both SQL and PartiQL, queries can be executed across structured and unstructured data, respectively. PartiQL, an extension of SQL, simplifies querying unstructured data like JSON by offering built-in functions for easy navigation and parsing. Additionally, you can continue using native SQL from structured data sources such as relational database systems, ensuring compatibility and ease of use across the entire dataset.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue to crawl the data sources and store the metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. With Amazon Athena, use SQL and PartiQL to query structured and semi-structured data respectively.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Utilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, saving metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Run SQL queries using Redshift spectrum</span></strong> is incorrect because it simply introduces more complexity and cost compared to Athena for querying across diverse data sources without the need for data transformation. Although Redshift Spectrum allows querying data in S3, it does not meet the requirements given in the scenario.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, with metadata stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Transform JSON format data to Apache Parquet or .csv using AWS Glue jobs, storing transformed data in S3. Query both original and transformed data using Amazon Athena </strong></span>is incorrect because this option primarily involves additional operational overhead for data transformation. It requires configuration for AWS Glue Jobs and handling data formats, which is unnecessary for the requirement to query data in its original format.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Configure Amazon RDS to directly query data using federated queries for RDS, DynamoDB, and S3. Store JSON and .csv data in S3 and use SQL Server-linked servers for cross-database queries</span></strong> is incorrect because Amazon RDS federated queries simply do not natively support querying across DynamoDB and S3 in the manner described. This option introduces significant complexity and misrepresents the capabilities of federated queries and SQL Server-linked servers in the context of AWS services.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheatsheet:<br>\n</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a><strong><br>\n</strong></p>"},{"id":36,"sourceNumber":53,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineering team is building a central data catalog and metadata management system for various data sources on AWS. The data includes semistructured and unstructured data formats, such as CSV logs, JSON files, .xml files, and NoSQL data in Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility). The team seeks an automated solution to continuously update the data catalog and monitor changes in source metadata with the least manual intervention.</p>\n<p>Which solution requires the LEAST amount of operational overhead to fulfill these requirements?</p>","answers":["Implement MongoDB as the data catalog. Create a Lambda function to collect and update metadata in MongoDB. Schedule the function to run at regular intervals.","Utilize the AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository. Set up Glue crawlers on a schedule to regularly crawl the data sources and refresh the metadata catalog.","Configure AWS Glue jobs to extract schema details and store the schema data in Amazon S3.","Utilize Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) as the data catalog and create AWS Lambda functions to collect and refresh metadata in the DocumentDB database. Set a cron job to execute the Lambda functions periodically."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>The <strong>AWS Glue Data Catalog</strong> is a fully managed, serverless, and cloud-optimized metadata repository that integrates seamlessly with other AWS services. It provides a unified view of all your data assets, making it easier to discover, search, and manage data across various AWS data stores.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/abd8cc17cd00627f5d1062ffc56ec3c06509dd1648b882f8496acba387460bd8.png\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue crawlers automatically scan your data sources, extract schema information, and populate the Glue Data Catalog with metadata tables, which can be used for data discovery, extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs, and query execution. This process significantly reduces manual efforts and ensures that your data catalog is always up-to-date with the latest schema changes.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Utilize the AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository. Set up Glue crawlers on a schedule to regularly crawl the data sources and refresh the metadata catalog.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Implement MongoDB as the data catalog. Create a Lambda function to collect and update metadata in MongoDB. Schedule the function to run at regular intervals</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This option&nbsp;requires custom development and manual setup for metadata management, which is less efficient than using AWS Glue's automated capabilities.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Configure AWS Glue jobs to extract schema details and store the schema data in Amazon S3&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. While this could work, it would require you to write custom scripts. This not only adds to the development effort but also to the maintenance effort, as any changes in data formats or structures would necessitate updates to your scripts. Glue jobs is more suitable for transformation jobs rather than inferring schema.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) as the data catalog and create AWS Lambda functions to collect and refresh metadata in the DocumentDB database. Set a cron job to execute the Lambda functions periodically</strong>&nbsp;</span>is incorrect. Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatiblity) is primarily a document database and not specialized for metadata management across various data sources. It would require more manual configuration and lacks the seamless integration with data cataloging features provided by AWS Glue.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/populate-data-catalog.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/populate-data-catalog.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/crawler-data-stores.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/crawler-data-stores.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":37,"sourceNumber":55,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A financial company is developing a data lake using Amazon S3. The company plans to utilize services such as Amazon Athena, Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR), and Amazon Redshift Spectrum to meet its data analysis needs.</p>\n<p>The company has several departments that need access to the data lake. Each department should only access parts of the data relevant to their work, with permissions set at both the column and row levels to meet their specific needs.</p>\n<p>How can the company meet this requirement in the LEAST amount of operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Utilize Amazon S3 for the data lake's storage and implement S3 access policies for row and column access restrictions. Facilitate data access directly via Amazon S3.","Utilize Apache Ranger through Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) to manage access at the row and column level. Integrate Amazon S3 for data lake storage and provide data access via Apache Pig.","Utilize Amazon Redshift for data lake storage with Redshift policies for access control. Access data via Apache Spark and Amazon Athena federated queries.","Utilize Amazon S3 for data lake storage and leverage AWS Lake Formation for row and column-level access control. Enable data access via AWS Lake Formation."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong><span>AWS Lake Formation</span></strong><span> is a central hub on Amazon S3 for storing structured and unstructured data until needed. It manages permissions and access for data stored in S3. Lake Formation provides granular control over data access and a unified Storage API for secure data operations. Key features include encryption, private endpoints, and audit logs for enhanced security and isolation of the data lake within S3.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8edbe54def93332c386526d8c54857c292f04df6de424de2e2cbe756efa4b58f.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Lake Formation also offers centralized data governance through the AWS Glue Data Catalog, employing row and column-level access controls for precise access management. This ensures fine-grained control over data accessibility. Amazon Athena and Redshift Spectrum can then query S3 data while adhering to Lake Formation's permissions, thereby maintaining proper access control. Apache Pig on Amazon ElasticMap Reduce can also leverage Lake Formation permissions to securely access data from the S3 data lake. Lastly, integration with Apache Ranger enables centralized access management, where Ranger policies are applied to data access via Apache Pig, and other tools.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon S3 for data lake storage and leverage AWS Lake Formation for row and column-level access control. Enable data access via AWS Lake Formation.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon S3 for the data lake's storage and implement S3 access policies for row and column access restrictions. Facilitate data access directly via Amazon S3</strong></span> is incorrect because S3 access policies alone cannot provide the granular row-level and column-level access control required by the scenario. While S3 offers robust data storage capabilities, it lacks the finer access controls available through AWS Lake Formation, which are essential for detailed data access management as described.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Apache Ranger through Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) to manage access at the row and column level. Integrate Amazon S3 for data lake storage and provide data access via Apache Pig</strong></span> is incorrect. Apache Ranger manages access in Hadoop environments but doesn't fully utilize AWS's capabilities for managing data lake security. AWS Lake Formation provides centralized access control across multiple services, making it a better choice.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon Redshift for data lake storage with Redshift policies for access control. Access data via Apache Spark and Amazon Athena federated queries</strong></span> is incorrect. This option is not aligned with the scenario's focus on building a data lake with Amazon S3 and managing access with AWS Lake Formation. Although Amazon Redshift is a powerful data warehousing solution, it is not intended to serve as the primary storage for a data lake. Furthermore, using Redshift for data lake storage would not provide the same level of granular access control or the ease of management offered by AWS Lake Formation when used in conjunction with Amazon S3.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet: </strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>"},{"id":38,"sourceNumber":56,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A company's data engineering team needs to process and analyze terabytes of IoT data stored in Amazon S3. The structure of the data changes periodically as often as the IoT device gets upgraded.</p>\n<p>The team is required to find a solution that includes data cataloging and schema management to ensure consistent access and analysis capabilities.</p>\n<p>Which option satisfies the mentioned requirements in the MOST cost-effective manner?</p>","answers":["Set up an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. Leverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in S3, and handle data ingestion via Redshift stored procedures.","Set up an Amazon Athena workgroup. Leverage Apache Spark through Athena for data exploration in S3, granting the team access to workgroup schemas and tables.","Set up an AWS Glue Data Catalog. Define a schema using AWS Glue Schema Registry, and orchestrate data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Glue workflows.","Set up AWS Glue Data Catalog, Schema Registry, and Lambda functions with Redshift Data API. Manage data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Step Functions."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue</strong> is a service that facilitates the extraction, transformation, and loading (ETL) of data to prepare it for analytics. It is fully managed and offers visual workflows along with a Python library to easily perform data transformations at scale.</p>\n<p>The <strong>AWS Glue Data Catalog</strong> is a centralized metadata repository that stores table definitions, physical locations of data, and business attributes and tracks change over time. It provides a unified interface to query metadata across data sources. When AWS Glue ETL jobs run, they use the data catalog to understand data formats and ensure correct transformations.</p>\n<p>The <strong>AWS Glue Schema Registry</strong> tracks schema changes to tables in the data catalog. It allows schemas to evolve over time while maintaining compatibility with existing analytics, queries, and applications. This enables schema-on-read for analytics tools like Athena, EMR, and Redshift Spectrum.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/3c3c3d7f0fbc04dd47e19feba0b79f38397c5efc94d5a3a53fd16a1a0dc8784c.png\"></p>\n<p><strong>Amazon Redshift Serverless</strong> is a cloud-based, fully managed data warehouse service that allows you to easily analyze your data using standard SQL and existing BI tools. It's designed to be fast and cost-effective, making it ideal for petabyte-scale data analysis.</p>\n<p>To meet the requirements for cost-effective data cataloging, schema management, and analytics, the recommended solution involves utilizing the AWS Glue Data Catalog for organizing and querying vast amounts of dynamic IoT data stored in S3, ensuring consistent access for analysis. The AWS Glue Schema Registry is employed to manage evolving schemas of IoT data in a version-controlled manner during device upgrades. AWS Glue workflows orchestrate the periodic ingestion of data from S3 into Amazon Redshift Serverless, enabling scalable analytics capabilities without the need for provisioning and managing dedicated Redshift clusters. With Redshift Serverless, customers benefit from a pay-for-what-you-use model, which is more cost-effective for handling large and fluctuating IoT datasets. This entire serverless solution leverages native AWS services, minimizing both cost and complexity by eliminating the need for additional infrastructure setup and maintenance.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Set up an AWS Glue Data Catalog. Define a schema using AWS Glue Schema Registry, and orchestrate data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Glue workflows.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. Leverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in S3, and handle data ingestion via Redshift stored procedures</strong></span> is incorrect. This option involves unnecessary complexity and higher costs associated with managing a provisioned cluster and does not leverage the serverless capabilities that can lead to cost savings.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up an Amazon Athena workgroup. Leverage Apache Spark through Athena for data exploration in S3, granting the team access to workgroup schemas and tables</strong></span> is incorrect. While Athena provides serverless querying capabilities, this option lacks the comprehensive data management and schema consistency features provided by AWS Glue Schema Registry and Data Catalog, which are essential for handling evolving data structures efficiently.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up AWS Glue Data Catalog, Schema Registry, and Lambda functions with Redshift Data API. Manage data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Step Functions</strong></span> is incorrect. This option introduces unnecessary components like AWS Lambda functions for data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless and AWS Step Functions for orchestration. While these services can be used, they require custom code development, maintenance, and incur additional costs for execution and data transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-glue.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating-redshift-connection.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating-redshift-connection.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue and Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":39,"sourceNumber":58,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer is trying to use AWS Glue Interactive Sessions in Amazon SageMaker Studio for data preparation tasks. The data engineer has successfully created a SageMaker IAM role with trust policies for both SageMaker and Glue services. The interactive session is being incorporated into an ML workflow that prepares data for subsequent analytical tasks. Despite this setup, the data engineer is being denied access when attempting to initiate an Glue Interactive Session.</p>\n<p>How can the data engineer resolve the issue?</p>","answers":["<span>Attach the <code>AmazonSageMakerFullAccess</code> managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.</span>","<span>Attach the <code>AwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole</code> managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.</span>","<span>Ensure the data engineer's IAM user has permission to perform the <code>sts:AssumeRole</code> and <code>sagemaker:AddAssociation</code> actions.</span>","<span>Add the <code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code> managed policy to the IAM user of the data engineer.</span>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Amazon SageMaker Studio is an integrated development environment (IDE) for machine learning that provides every tool needed to build, train, and deploy machine learning models. SageMaker offers a user-friendly interface for executing ML workflows, making its tools accessible across various integrated development environments (IDEs). Additionally, It conducts operations on your behalf by leveraging other AWS services. You need to assign permissions to enable SageMaker to utilize these services and access the corresponding resources. This permission is achieved through an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) execution role, granting SageMaker the necessary permissions.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5f9a876af9a6e759a735c721d871167ff49a330cf58f56bb6561f9c382016743.png\" alt=\"Amazon SageMaker console Setting up an IAM role using Role Manager\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue Interactive Sessions provides a programmatic and visual interface for building and testing ETL scripts for data preparation. To use it in Amazon SageMaker Studio, the SageMaker execution role needs to have the necessary permissions for AWS Glue. This can be achieved by attaching the <code>AwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole</code> managed policy to the SageMaker execution role.</p>\n<p>The <code>AwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole</code> policy grants the required permissions for creating and managing AWS Glue Interactive Sessions, as well as accessing the AWS Glue Data Catalog and other related resources.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Attach the <code>AwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole</code> managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong> Attach the <code>AmazonSageMakerFullAccess</code> managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;This policy just grants all actions across Amazon SageMaker, which is not required for the specific task of using AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Ensure the data engineer's IAM user has permission to perform the <code>sts:AssumeRole</code> and <code>sagemaker:AddAssociation</code> actions</strong></span> is incorrect. While&nbsp;<code>sts:AssumeRole</code>&nbsp;is required to assume an IAM role, this permission is not specifically needed for using AWS Glue Interactive Sessions. Additionally, the <code>sagemaker:AddAssociation</code> permission is only used for associating resources with a SageMaker notebook instance or studio, but it's not directly related to AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Add the <code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code> managed policy to the IAM user of the data engineer</span></strong> is incorrect because this policy is typically used to grant AWS Glue the permissions it needs to execute jobs and does not apply to the SageMaker execution role's permissions for AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/getting-started-glue-sm.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/getting-started-glue-sm.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-roles.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-roles.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these AWS Glue and Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/</a></p>"},{"id":40,"sourceNumber":59,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineering team manages an Amazon DynamoDB table configured with provisioned capacity mode. The table uses a primary key to uniquely identify the items stored by the application. The table experiences a highly predictable traffic pattern, with peaks occurring at midnight every day. These peaks are followed by a period of low activity in the morning, which gradually transitions to normal levels for the remainder of the day.</p>\n<p>The data engineering team wants to maintain the application's performance during peak times and save costs during low usage periods.</p>\n<p>Which solution meets this requirement?</p>","answers":["Set the provisioned capacity to match the highest observed demand during peak periods.","Update the table’s class to DynamoDB Standard Infrequent Access (DynamoDB Standard-IA).","Update the table’s capacity to use AWS Application Auto Scaling based on minimum and maximum utilization.","Switch to an on-demand capacity mode for the table."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon DynamoDB</strong> allows for two capacity modes for managing throughput costs: provisioned and on-demand. In provisioned capacity mode, the user specifies the number of reads and writes per second as required by the application. This mode is cost-effective for predictable workloads where the throughput does not frequently change. On-demand capacity mode, on the other hand, offers flexible billing and automatically accommodates the table's workload without requiring manual scaling.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS Application Auto Scaling</strong> enables automatic adjustment of resources to maintain consistent, predictable performance at the lowest possible cost. It supports scheduling, which allows for capacity planning based on known usage patterns. This feature is particularly useful for applications with predictable load patterns.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/895a982dbd4b418dc1aed971dc09ca501d759fa9cb2a5863bc49abe835291edf.png\" alt=\"Table capacity configuration for Amazon DynamoDB auto scaling settings\"></p>\n<p>Implementing <strong>AWS Application Auto Scaling for DynamoDB</strong> allows for the dynamic adjustment of provisioned capacity to align with the application’s workload patterns, ensuring high performance during peak times and cost savings during off-peak times. This approach leverages the predictable nature of the workload, enhancing resource utilization efficiency without the need for manual intervention or the costs associated with maintaining excessive provisioned capacity.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Update the table’s capacity to use AWS Application Auto Scaling based on minimum and maximum utilization.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set the provisioned capacity to match the highest observed demand during peak periods</strong></span> is incorrect.&nbsp;In a provisioned capacity mode, you pay for the specified capacity regardless of actual usage. Therefore, matching capacity to peak demand without adjusting for periods of low activity only results in paying for unneeded capacity during those lower periods, which is not cost-effective.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Update the table’s class to DynamoDB Standard Infrequent Access (DynamoDB Standard-IA)</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;DynamoDB Standard-IA is primarily used in scenarios where data is accessed less frequently, offering lower storage costs while maintaining the same performance levels as the standard storage class. However, reading from and writing to these tables costs more than the Standard class, making it pricier for tables that are frequently used, such as the one in the scenario.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Switch to an on-demand capacity mode for the table</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. The on-demand capacity mode charges for each read or write, unlike the provisioned mode, where you pay for a set capacity. For tables with predictable traffic, using provisioned capacity often works out cheaper. This is because you can set your capacity to cover the expected throughput, avoiding the costs tied to the per-action pricing of the on-demand mode.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/application/userguide/what-is-application-auto-scaling.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/application/userguide/what-is-application-auto-scaling.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon DynamoDB Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/</a></p>"},{"id":41,"sourceNumber":60,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p><span>A data engineer needs to perform a one-time, ad-hoc query to retrieve specific columns from a large dataset stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The dataset contains 25 columns in Apache Parquet format and has been registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. The solution must be efficient, serverless, and avoid introducing operational overhead or requiring infrastructure management. </span></p>\n<p><span>Which solution will best meet these requirements?</span></p>","answers":["<span>Create a Lambda function to read S3 data and format it in a pandas dataframe. Use Python to run a <code>SQL SELECT</code> statement on the dataframe to query specific columns.</span>","<span>\nUtilize Amazon Athena to perform <code>SQL SELECT</code> queries to fetch specific columns.</span>","Configure an AWS Glue DataBrew project to ingest Apache Parquet files from the S3 bucket and perform column-specific queries.","Configure an S3 Object Lambda access point that filters and retrieves only the necessary columns from the Parquet files."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Athena</strong> is a user-friendly query service that allows users to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 by using familiar SQL queries. It operates in a serverless environment, which means there's no need to manage any infrastructure, and users only pay for the queries they execute. This makes Athena a highly efficient and cost-effective solution for ad-hoc queries or one-time operations, as it automatically scales resources based on demand.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/70c00cdaad24a2bff3f7c221e72a554b34595eb14d63ff6510b348a138d21449.png\" alt=\"Athena SELECT Column Query\"></p>\n<p>By utilizing Amazon Athena, a data engineer can efficiently retrieve specific columns from a large Apache Parquet dataset stored in Amazon S3 without introducing additional operational overhead. This is particularly beneficial since Athena can query data directly from S3, eliminating the need for data loading or complex ETL processes. This feature significantly reduces the effort and time typically associated with moving or copying data to another storage or processing system.</p>\n<p>Moreover, Athena's native support for the Apache Parquet columnar format makes it an ideal choice for analytical workloads. Parquet is optimized for retrieving specific columns, allowing data engineers to access the information they need quickly and efficiently. Additionally, since Athena uses standard SQL syntax, data engineers and analysts who are already familiar with SQL can easily query and analyze the data without having to learn a new query language or framework.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon Athena to perform <code>SQL SELECT</code> queries to fetch specific columns.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Create an AWS Lambda function to read S3 data and format it in a pandas dataframe. Use Python to run a <code>SQL SELECT</code> statement on the dataframe to query specific columns</span></strong> is incorrect. This method requires setting up a Lambda function and writing code, which introduces operational overhead and may not be suitable for a one-time query operation. Additionally, processing large datasets within a Lambda function may not be efficient due to the typically limited resources and execution time constraints of Lambda functions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure a Glue DataBrew project to ingest Apache Parquet files from the S3 bucket and perform column-specific queries</strong></span> is incorrect. While DataBrew can ingest Parquet files from S3 and perform column-specific queries, configuring a project involves multiple steps and generating recipes. For a one-time column retrieval from Parquet in S3, DataBrew's capabilities will only introduce unnecessary complexity and overhead compared to more direct solutions like Athena SQL queries.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an S3 Object Lambda access point that filters and retrieves only the necessary columns from the Parquet files&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. While S3 Object Lambda could retrieve specific columns, it typically serves for data transformation rather than querying specific columns directly and is not the most straightforward approach for ad-hoc querying.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/how-to-optimize-querying-your-data-in-amazon-s3/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/how-to-optimize-querying-your-data-in-amazon-s3/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon S3 and Amazon Athena following Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"><span>https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</span></a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>"},{"id":42,"sourceNumber":61,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A gaming company seeks a system to monitor player activities and interactions across various online platforms in real-time. The desired system must be capable of ingesting streaming data from game events formatted as nested JSON. The system must also efficiently handle a high volume of I/O requests generated by continuous player activity and game events. It must also keep the query latency under single-digit milliseconds to provide quick updates for game dashboards and analytics.</p>\n<p>How can the company minimize operational overhead while meeting these requirements effectively?</p>","answers":["Ingest event data to a self-managed Apache Kafka hosted in Amazon EC2 instances. Analyze the data with Amazon Redshift.","Publish the data to an Amazon Aurora Serverless database and use native SQL queries.","Ingest event data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create an application for consuming data and loading results in Amazon DynamoDB for querying.","Publish game event data to an Amazon SQS queue. Create an AWS Lambda function to process data in batches."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Kinesis Data</strong> Streams is a service that continuously captures and stores streaming data records in real-time. It can ingest massive amounts of data from hundreds of sources, such as application logs, social media feeds, website clickstreams, etc.</p>\n<p>- Producers like applications, servers, devices, etc., can continuously send data records to a data stream in Kinesis.</p>\n<p>- Consumers, like custom applications, can then process/analyze the streaming data by reading data records from the stream.</p>\n<p>- Applications can use the Kinesis Client Library for reading data from streams.</p>\n<p>Processed records can then be stored in other AWS services like DynamoDB, Redshift, S3, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon DynamoDB</strong> is a fully managed NoSQL database that offers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. It is a document and key-value database that provides predictable performance with seamless scalability.</p>\n<p>- It allows data to be stored and queried in JSON format, which makes it suitable for storing processed records from Kinesis streams.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0537ad1242f42db026dbd12ea851c3949faccd08090514a97f914a7834d4ebcb.png\" alt=\"Data stream analytics with Managed Apache Flink\"></p>\n<p>To best fulfill the gaming company's requirements, it's recommended that Amazon Kinesis Data Streams be utilized to ingest game event data. With its ability to handle streaming data from numerous sources at high throughput, Kinesis Data Streams is an ideal choice for this task. Subsequently, storing the results in Amazon DynamoDB allows for efficient querying. DynamoDB's capability to store data in nested JSON format aligns well with the needs of the project, while its fast and predictable performance ensures swift queries. With DynamoDB being a fully managed NoSQL database service, latency for queries can be maintained under 10 milliseconds, meeting the necessary performance criteria.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Ingest event data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create an application for consuming data and loading results in Amazon DynamoDB for querying.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Ingest event data to a self-managed Apache Kafka hosted in Amazon EC2 instances. Analyze the data with Amazon Redshift&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect.&nbsp;This solution would simply require managing Kafka clusters on EC2 instances, which adds operational overhead. Additionally, Redshift is better suited for batch analytics rather than low-latency queries.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Publish the data to an Amazon Aurora Serverless database and use native SQL queries</strong></span> is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora Serverless can automatically scale, it is primarily a relational database service, which makes it less suitable for handling nested JSON data and low-latency queries at scale.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Publish game event data to an Amazon SQS queue. Create an AWS Lambda function to process data in batches&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect.&nbsp;Although SQS queues are great for decoupling components, they introduce latency that may prevent the single-digit millisecond response times from being achieved. Moreover, processing data in batches could further delay the availability of data for real-time analysis and dashboard updates.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/kds.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/kds.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/real-time-data-streaming/\">https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/real-time-data-streaming/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon Kinesis and Amazon DynamoDB Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/</a></p>"},{"id":43,"sourceNumber":62,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>An organization is migrating its Apache Hadoop cluster from a data center to an Amazon EMR cluster. The organization wants to adopt a serverless architecture for managing its data catalog, which is currently managed through an Apache Hive metastore within its local Hadoop cluster. The metastore records must be stored in persistent data storage. The organization is also reviewing its existing Apache Oozie workflows as part of the broader migration from the on-premises Hadoop environment.</p>\n<p>What is the MOST cost-effective solution to fulfill these requirements?</p>","answers":["Migrate the on-premises Hive metastore to Amazon S3 using AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS).","Create AWS Data Catalog tables as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster.","Configure an Amazon Aurora MySQL database as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster.","Launch a new Hive metastore in the EMR cluster and transfer existing on-premises data to it. Configure AWS DataSync for ongoing replication of the EMR Hive metastore changes to an Amazon S3 bucket."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>In an Amazon EMR cluster, Hive records metastore information in a MySQL database on the primary node's file system by default. The metastore contains a description of the table and the underlying data on which it is built, including the partition names, data types, and so on. When a cluster terminates, all cluster nodes shut down, including the primary node. When this happens, local data is lost because node file systems use ephemeral storage. If you need the metastore to persist, you must create an&nbsp;<strong>external metastore</strong>&nbsp;that exists outside the cluster.</p>\n<p>You have two options for an external metastore:</p>\n<p><strong>1. AWS Glue Data Catalog</strong></p>\n<p><strong>2. Amazon RDS or Amazon Aurora</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ea518a13e89bb402f9570db7c72a5b59b14a2bdc4bf585a8d0b3d560f890e110.png\" alt=\"Migrating Hadoop to EMR and Hive Metastore to AWS\"></p>\n<p>The AWS Glue Data Catalog provides a centralized metadata repository for clients running Apache Hive and Apache Spark. This serverless repository eliminates the need for operational tasks like scaling, patching, and managing servers, which aligns well with the organization’s goal to adopt a serverless architecture. The AWS Glue Data Catalog can seamlessly integrate with Amazon EMR, providing a managed Hive metastore service that simplifies the process of cataloging, searching, and querying metadata.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create AWS Data Catalog tables as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Migrate the on-premises Hive metastore to Amazon S3 using AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon S3 can store Hive data, but using it as a standalone metastore is not just standard practice. This is because S3, as an object storage service, lacks native support for database-like operations, schema management, and querying capabilities that are typical of a metastore. Metastores depend on structured schema information to manage and query metadata about the data stored, usually in relational databases or specialized metadata management services like the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an Amazon Aurora MySQL database as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora MySQL is a valid external metastore, it&nbsp;requires managing a database instance, which contradicts the requirement of a serverless solution.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Launch a new Hive metastore in the EMR cluster and transfer existing on-premises data to it. Configure AWS DataSync for ongoing replication of the EMR Hive metastore changes to an Amazon S3 bucket</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Local Hive metastores within EMR clusters are vulnerable to data loss during node failures, making them not a persistent storage solution. Moreover, AWS DataSync can only monitor and synchronize file changes, not database changes.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon Aurora and Amazon EMR Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/</a></p>"},{"id":44,"sourceNumber":63,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineering team manages 10 TB of data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The team must meet new data lifecycle and retention policies. Under the new guidelines, all newly stored data must be readily available for random, occasional SQL queries. Data older than 3 years requires secure storage but must be accessible within 8 hours for compliance audits. Additionally, data exceeding a 10-year retention period should be permanently deleted. The team initially explored using a tumbling window for real-time analytics but determined that occasional SQL queries made a simpler, cost-effective approach more appropriate.</p>\n<p>Which solution will fulfill this requirement most cost-effectively?</p>","answers":["Configure the new data to be stored in the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class. Use Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deletion after 10 years.","Set the S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class for new data with a configuration towards Deep Archive Access for longer-term storage. Utilize Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Configure S3 Intelligent Tiering to purge data after 10 years.","Use Amazon Redshift for data storage of the new data, then configure Redshift Spectrum to directly query data on S3. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to the Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class. Set up another lifecycle rule to delete data longer than 10 years.","Configure Amazon RDS to store the newly added data. Create an Amazon Lambda function to periodically export the snapshots to the S3 Standard class. Set up lifecycle policies for transferring snapshots to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deleting data after 10 years."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon S3</strong> offers different types of storage classes that users can choose based on the specific needs of their workload. Depending on their workload's data access, resiliency, and performance requirements, these classes are tailored to provide the best storage options at the lowest possible costs. S3 storage classes are versatile and suitable for nearly any use case - including those with high-performance demands, data lakes, residency requirements, unknown or changing access patterns, or archival storage.</p>\n<p>One of these classes is the Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA). Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but, when needed, requires rapid access. S3 Standard-IA offers the high durability, throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard but with a lower storage cost and a slightly higher retrieval fee. This storage class makes it an ideal solution for long-term storage and backups and a data store for disaster recovery files. Amazon Athena allows users to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 with SQL queries. It's interactive and serverless, so there's no infrastructure to manage. Users are charged only for the queries they run, making it an incredibly cost-effective solution.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/cb434042f543ea5c8acbd790bd85ffead1c710a64cb5da3986acee1cc869ec41.png\" alt=\"S3 lifecycle rule deleting noncurrent objects\"></p>\n<p>By implementing lifecycle rules in Amazon S3, you can easily manage your data throughout its lifecycle. S3 Lifecycle configuration rules allow you to transition objects to more cost-effective storage classes, such as S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval or S3 Glacier Deep Archive, for long-term storage and archiving purposes. Moreover, you have the option to set up rules that will automatically delete objects after a specific period, which helps you comply with data governance and regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, previously known as S3 Glacier, is a cost-effective storage solution for archiving data. You can configure retrieval times from minutes to hours, which is suitable for accessing data for compliance audits within a 10-hour window. On the other hand, the S3 Glacier Deep Archive is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but needs to be retained for decades as part of digital preservation or regulatory compliance. It is the most affordable long-term archiving storage option available on AWS.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Configure the new data to be stored in the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class. Use Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deletion after 10 years.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set the S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class for new data with a configuration towards Deep Archive Access for longer-term storage. Utilize Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Configure S3 Intelligent Tiering to purge data after 10 years</strong></span> is incorrect.&nbsp;Although using Amazon Athena to query data meets one of the requirements, transitioning to Deep Archive Access makes it impossible for data to be accessible within an 8-hour window. S3 Glacier Deep Archive is intended for long-term data storage that is accessed once or twice a year, with retrieval times typically ranging from 12 to 48 hours.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon Redshift for data storage of the new data, then configure Redshift Spectrum to directly query data on S3. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to the Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class. Set up another lifecycle rule to delete data longer than 10 years</strong></span> is incorrect. Redshift is primarily a data warehousing solution for large-scale analytical workloads, not occasional SQL queries on recently ingested data. While Redshift Spectrum allows querying data in S3, maintaining a Redshift cluster incurs hourly charges, making it a less cost-effective solution. Moreover, although S3 Glacier Deep Archive is cheaper than Glacier Flexible Retrieval, it only allows retrieval times ranging from 12 to 48 hours.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Configure Amazon RDS to store the newly added data. Create an Amazon Lambda function to periodically export the snapshots to the S3 Standard class. Set up lifecycle policies for transferring snapshots to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deleting data after 10 years</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;Amazon RDS is primarily a database service for relational databases and is not optimized for cost-effective data storage and retrieval for compliance and lifecycle management in the way S3 and its associated services are. Moreover, managing snapshots and exports introduces operational overhead that does not directly contribute to the goals of easy SQL analysis and compliance with lifecycle policies.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/backup-recovery/amazon-s3-glacier.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/backup-recovery/amazon-s3-glacier.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these&nbsp;Amazon S3 Glacier and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-glacier/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-glacier/</a></p>"},{"id":45,"sourceNumber":64,"previouslyWrong":false,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineer is using a MySQL database as a central metadata store. This database holds Hive records, which serve as the schema for SQL queries run in Amazon Athena and processed on Amazon EMR. The current Hadoop-based environment also runs Apache Ranger on an Amazon EC2 instance to manage cluster policies. The data engineer wants to migrate the metadata store to a solution requiring less operational effort.</p>\n<p>Which would meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["Provision AWS Lake Formation together with EMR.","Leverage the local Hive metastore on an EMR cluster.","Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository.","Deploy an external Hive metastore using an Amazon Aurora MySQL database instance."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>The <strong>AWS Glue Data Catalog</strong> is a central metadata repository, encompassing structural and operational metadata for data assets across various data sources. It provides a consolidated interface for maintaining and accessing information about data formats, schemas, and origins. When running an AWS Glue ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) job, this catalog is employed to understand data details, ensuring the precision of the data transformation process.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/abde69738842a7c1753498f206840899d623a33298d617482940fd6f9c96ea25.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue console Setting table properties in Data Catalog\"></p>\n<p>In the scenario, by using AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository, you eliminate the need to manage separate metastores or manually configure connections, reducing operational overhead. As a managed service, AWS Glue Data Catalog provides scalability, reliability, and security out of the box, further decreasing the administrative effort required from data engineers.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Provision AWS Lake Formation together with EMR</span></strong> is incorrect because AWS Lake Formation is just used to simplify and secure your data lake. While it integrates with Amazon EMR and provides capabilities for managing metadata, its primary focus is simply on setting up, securing, and managing data lakes rather than serving as a dedicated metadata repository.&nbsp;It's more about data lake governance and less about being a direct solution for managing Hive metadata specifically.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Leverage the local Hive metastore on an EMR cluster</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach ties the metadata management closely to the lifecycle of the EMR cluster. This would require managing the EMR cluster's scaling, security, and availability, which can increase operational effort rather than reduce it.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Deploy an external Hive metastore using an Amazon Aurora MySQL database instance</strong></span> is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora offers an improved, managed database solution compared to standard MySQL installations with high availability and scalability, it still requires operational effort in terms of database management, scaling, and maintenance. This option might reduce some operational efforts but does not fully leverage the serverless and integrated capabilities of AWS services designed for metadata management.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"},{"id":46,"sourceNumber":65,"previouslyWrong":true,"section":"Time Mode 1 · Câu bổ sung","question":"<p>A data engineering team needs to develop an ETL(Extract, Transform, and Load) pipeline to handle data inflow from ten distinct source systems. These systems generate files in CSV, JSON, and Apache Parquet formats. These files, varying in size from 5 MB to 15 GB, are stored in a single Amazon S3 bucket every 20 minutes.</p>\n<p>The pipeline must dynamically adjust to schema changes while processing and loading the data into ten specific tables in an Amazon Redshift database.</p>\n<p>Which solutions will MOST effectively meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Initiate an AWS Glue job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge Schedule. Process the data with an Apache Spark DataFrame and load it into Amazon Redshift tables.","Initiate an AWS Glue workflow job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge rules. Use a Glue crawler to detect schema modifications and then process and load data into Amazon Redshift with Apache Spark DataFrame after schema validation.","Create two AWS Lambda functions: one to trigger a Glue crawler upon new S3 file uploads and another to execute a Glue job processing data with Apache Spark DataFrame and loading it into Amazon Redshift. The second function is coordinated by an Amazon EventBridge rule after the crawler's successful execution.","Initiate a Glue workflow through an S3 event notification with an AWS Lambda function. Trigger an on-demand Glue crawler followed by a Glue job using Apache Spark DataFrame to process and load data into Amazon Redshift upon schema detection.","Use an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Hive to process the data every 20 minutes and load it into Amazon Redshift tables."],"correct":[1,3],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue workflows</strong> enable the orchestration of complex ETL tasks by linking multiple jobs and crawlers, allowing for automated data processing sequences. These workflows can be initiated based on predefined schedules or in response to specific events, facilitating flexible and efficient data management strategies.</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon EventBridge</strong> (formerly CloudWatch Events) is a serverless event bus service that connects AWS services, applications, and devices. Using simple rules, events in one service can be used to trigger actions in another.</p>\n<p><strong>Apache Spark DataFrames</strong> are distributed data collections arranged into named columns that enable parallel SQL operations on massive datasets. Glue jobs can use Spark DataFrames to process and convert data.</p>\n<p><strong>S3 event notifications</strong> allow you to execute a Lambda function anytime an object is created or updated in an S3 bucket. Once new data arrives in S3, this can be used to start a Glue process for near-real-time ETL.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/1efa1848d968b8d58dcb803ebd806b3f92fdd444323dcacb5f79eea4c9e2641c.png\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue is a managed ETL service that facilitates the preparation and loading of data for analytics. It can automatically discover the schema of the data through Glue crawlers, making it ideal for environments where the data schema can change over time. The integration with Amazon EventBridge or S3 event notifications allows for automating these ETL jobs in response to data arriving in S3, adhering to the requirement of handling files every 20 minutes. Processing data with Apache Spark DataFrame before loading it into Amazon Redshift is an efficient method for handling large datasets and transforming them as needed.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Initiate an AWS Glue workflow job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge rules. Use a Glue crawler to detect schema modifications and then process and load data into Amazon Redshift with Apache Spark DataFrame after schema validation.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Initiate a Glue workflow through an S3 event notification with an AWS Lambda function. Trigger an on-demand Glue crawler followed by a Glue job using Apache Spark DataFrame to process and load data into Amazon Redshift upon schema detection.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Initiate an AWS Glue job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge Schedule. Process the data with an Apache Spark DataFrame and load it into Amazon Redshift tables </strong></span>is incorrect because it primarily lacks dynamic schema adaptation. While leveraging AWS Glue and Amazon EventBridge for periodic data processing and loading, this option does not include a mechanism like a Glue crawler for detecting and adapting to schema changes in the source data. Schema changes are critical in this scenario, and by handling them dynamically, the ETL process could succeed when unexpected changes occur.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong> Create two AWS Lambda functions: one to trigger a Glue crawler upon new S3 file uploads and another to execute a Glue job processing data with Apache Spark DataFrame and loading it into Amazon Redshift. The second function is coordinated by an Amazon EventBridge rule after the crawler's successful execution </strong></span>is incorrect because it only introduces unnecessary complexity and potential points of failure. While this solution addresses schema changes using a Glue crawler, coordinating multiple AWS Lambda functions adds complexity and could lead to inefficiencies. The process might be more streamlined and reliable than directly using Glue workflows, which are designed to handle such ETL tasks more elegantly.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Hive to process the data every 20 minutes and load it into Amazon Redshift tables</strong></span> is incorrect because it does not efficiently address dynamic schema changes and involves more complex setup and maintenance compared to AWS Glue-based solutions.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Tutorials Dojo Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>"}]};